window.harnessChunks["rows-89706aef79cad222.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"6\" data-a=\"6\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T23:45:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/humbertobellor/henterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Henterprise</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"6\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>6</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/humbertobellor/henterprise/tree/193e8fdac0d17abf833ab68e5179b273a6084e4c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 193e8fdac0d17abf833ab68e5179b273a6084e4c\">193e8fd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: departmental Hermes agents directly produce domain-specific enterprise outcomes using the supplied skills and personas. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific business requests, domain evidence, constraints, trade-offs, and tool/environment observations within each department&#39;s remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive domain reasoning, recommendation, analysis, or action needed to produce the requested bounded outcome. - Decision owner: the Hermes agent actor running the relevant Henterprise profile/skill. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hermes skill discovery and execution, departmental SOUL.md, skill procedures, related-skill references, and verification sections. - Closure path: request enters the agent loop; Henterprise selects/bounds the departmental capability; the agent produces and returns the domain outcome to the user or invoking work context. - Boundary reachability: documented full-tree Executive deployment and native delegation both expose departmental work skills to Hermes agent actors in the shipped Henterprise tree; separate departmental profiles are also explicitly installable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the substantive domain judgment remains model-driven and discretionary within the supplied remit; Henterprise&#39;s Markdown contracts shape that discretion but do not deterministically produce the answer. - Evidence: top-level README and departmental souls, including technology/SOUL.md and executive/SOUL.md. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Henterprise depends on Hermes Agent for the underlying autonomous execution loop and is therefore assessed as an organizational harness layer over that execution host.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen real cross-department handoff/dependency oscillation and incompatible shared contracts between separately executing Henterprise worker children. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unaccepted provider commitments, consumers acting on stale dates or assumptions, conflicting write surfaces, and incompatible changes to a shared API/data contract. - Distinct S1 units: separate Hermes delegatetask departmental worker children (for example Technology implementing a contract and Operations/Product consuming it), each with a real departmental remit and separate task context; not two skill names in one conversation. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a consuming worker proceeds on a commitment never accepted by its provider, or a provider revises a shared contract while a consumer continues under the stale version; two builders may also collide on the same write surface. - Attenuating coordination relation: the agentic Executive/orchestrator maintains a task-scoped commitment ledger, sends a proposed commitment to the provider child, requires an explicit accept | revise | reject response and agreed date, marks affected consumers blocked on a changed contract and reconciles, serializes conflicting write surfaces, or escalates to PMO. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted/revised provider response is included in each affected consumer&#39;s next brief before it resumes; rejection/escalation holds dependent work and may trigger re-dispatch with a reconciled contract. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these agent decisions address the specific inter-S1 failure of one-sided commitments, stale consumer assumptions and mutually incompatible simultaneous edits; message transport and task scheduling merely implement the selected coordination response. - Decisive decision or feedback right: provider agent owns accept/revise/reject on its contribution; the Executive agent owns reconciliation, blockade/serialization or escalation when shared assumptions conflict. The two decisions are complementary and actually affect dependent child work. - Decision owner: distinct autonomous provider and Executive Hermes agent actors following the first-party Henterprise parallel-agent-delivery and PMO instructions in the supported native task-scoped delegation mode. Hermes&#39; transport, response decoding and execution scheduling do not select these substantive commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic Hermes delegatetask, child brief/return contract, Executive-held task-scoped ledger/table, status blocking and re-dispatch instructions; no built-in persistent cross-profile database is implied. - Closure path: first departmental child requests dependency → second child responds accept/revise/reject → Executive updates ledger and resolves conflicting commitments → new provider decision/contract is returned to affected consumer child in its next brief → consumer&#39;s subsequent operation follows it (or remains blocked). - Boundary reachability: README.md explicitly enables task-scoped native delegation in one installed Executive profile; technology/parallel-agent-delivery/SKILL.md supplies the first-party decision/return procedure for actual child agents rather than an external demo or developer-only composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the Executive and provider agent judgments leaves Hermes with a generic delegation API and a static Markdown procedure, not the substantive commitment negotiation and conflict resolution. The agentic owners therefore have decisive S2 discretion in the documented, explicitly selected standard distribution mode. - Evidence: README.md, technology/parallel-agent-delivery/SKILL.md, and pmo/dependency-and-risk-management/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit for shipped procedure, structural for its scoped decision/return design; not observed runtime execution. - Confidence: medium; reviewer should independently verify mode reachability and action/feedback sufficiency. - Caveats: the separate persistent per-department-profile mode has no supplied cross-profile transport and must not borrow task-scoped closure. Henterprise does not ship its own delegatetask implementation; the specific Hermes build/version in use and actual child execution/return traces were not tested. No claim of reliable distributed transactional consistency or real-time perpetual coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current organization-wide commitments, portfolio capacity, priorities, and intervention on behalf of the whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: portfolio overcommitment, excessive work in progress, competing initiatives, resource contention, recurring execution failures, and cross-functional priority conflicts. - Whole-system current view: the PMO is explicitly tasked with making organization-wide delivery capacity visible; the Chief Executive receives cross-functional considerations and owns allocation of capital and attention. - Current-control decision scope: decide which work proceeds, draw the line at actual capacity, stop/pause/redirect work at stage gates, resolve resource contention, and arbitrate present cross-functional trade-offs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which current commitments the organization carries and which are displaced, stopped, or redirected. - Decision owner: the autonomous Hermes actor applying the PMO/COO/Chief Executive role appropriate to the current-control issue. - A witness mode: the documented one-profile-for-the-whole-organization deployment, where the same autonomous Hermes actor can load the complete Henterprise tree under the Executive organization-wide soul. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage-gate procedures, capacity measurement, portfolio records, operating cadence, owned artifacts and escalation conventions. - Closure path: whole-system capacity/commitment evidence → model-driven PMO/COO/Executive decision → stop/pause/redirect/displace/priority consequence → subsequent work in the same organization-wide agent context proceeds under that decision. - Boundary reachability: the documented full-tree Executive profile can load PMO/COO/Executive methods within one autonomous context; task-scoped delegation adds current child results but is not needed to borrow S3 ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the decisive portfolio/current-control judgment is model-driven and the supported single-profile mode does not require the developer to compose another actor or transport layer for the decision to govern subsequent work. The alternative multi-profile mode may need additional transport/state without negating the closed first-party witness mode. - Evidence: top-level README.md; pmo/head-of-pmo/SKILL.md; pmo/portfolio-governance/SKILL.md; operations/chief-operating-officer/SKILL.md; and executive/chief-executive/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: resource contention at portfolio level is S3 evidence here; S2 above is established separately through cross-unit dependency/handoff regulation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide complementary access to real produced artifacts beyond the operational producer&#39;s account, with independent reviewer judgment and feedback into subsequent control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-review bias, inaccurate security/legal claims, mutated artifacts during review and premature clearance of blocking findings. - Claim being audited: the builder&#39;s security/legal/compliance or other task result is sound enough to accept or progress. - Ordinary reporting path: a producer departmental/builder child creates the actual artifact and returns its own task result to the Executive. - Complementary access path: the Executive spawns a new distinct delegatetask reviewer child with the underlying artifact/workspace locator, acceptance criteria and relevant reviewer-class skill; the reviewer inspects primary evidence rather than merely trusting the producer&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: producer and reviewer have distinct child execution contexts and remits; reviewer is read-only, must not repair the artifact, and cannot be the original producer or the Executive role-playing a second opinion. The Executive verifies that the reviewed artifact revision did not change. This is task-local reviewer independence, not a claim of persistent separately hosted department profiles. - Who acts on findings: the reviewer agent decides clean | non-blocking | blocking with evidence; the Executive/producer must correct blocking findings and commission a fresh reviewer child, or route explicit named risk acceptance to the Executive via PMO rules without erasing the finding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent reviewer chooses the factual/risk verdict on primary artifacts. The separate Executive actor owns the S3 response, but must not silently rewrite or allow the producer to self-clear the reviewer finding. - Decision owner: distinct delegated reviewer Hermes agent for complementary audit discretion; Executive Hermes agent for routing, fresh review/return and exceptional named risk acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegatetask fresh child context; artifact locator/revision/checksum or pre-review diff; reviewer-class skill; optional outputschema as response checking (not semantic verdict); no-change check and correction/re-review procedure. - Closure path: builder artifact → fresh reviewer independently inspects primary artifact → evidence-bearing clean/non-blocking/blocking finding → Executive blocks or sends correction to builder (or obtains named risk acceptance) → corrected artifact reaches a new reviewer and only then subsequent builder/integration proceeds under the disposition. - Boundary reachability: README.md names the first-party task-scoped delegation mode; executive/agent-hierarchy/SKILL.md explicitly requires fresh native reviewer children with independent raw-evidence inspection and a return/feedback rule, all reachable from the installed Executive session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous reviewer rather than a deterministic parser owns the substantive independent finding; the structural no-change/response checks are supporting enforcement, not proxies for independent judgment. - Evidence: README.md, executive/agent-hierarchy/SKILL.md, and security/security-architecture-review/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit for shipped independent review protocol; structural for an actual separate reviewer child/return path; not execution-replayed proof. - Confidence: medium; independent reviewer should verify that this is a supported operational Henterprise mode rather than optional free-form user composition. - Caveats: instructions, not a hardcoded automatic review engine. The no-mutation check is procedural, not enforced by Henterprise tests; reviewers may share Executive task resources. No first-party durable routing of findings between persistent departmental profiles is shown. Runtime delegatetask behavior and end-to-end producer → reviewer → correction → fresh reviewer run have not been exercised for this exact ref.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model external and prospective conditions, develop adaptation options, and return them into present organizational capability and allocation decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing markets, competitors, regulation, technology, partnerships, acquisition opportunities, uncertain multi-year conditions, and falsified strategic assumptions. - External distinction: markets, segments, competitors, regulation, technology shifts, strategic partnerships, acquisitions/divestitures, and other conditions outside current internal execution. - Future / prospective distinction: multi-year horizon, scenario quadrants, load-bearing uncertainties, early-warning indicators, and assumptions whose falsification should change the strategy. - Adaptation option generated: robust moves, contingent moves, real options, market/business entry or exit choices, build/buy/partner choices, and portfolio recommendations. - Path back into current capability / S3: selected strategic option enters the same Executive/Finance agent decision context to alter current resource allocation, commitments or capability; scenario planning requires a present action and a review trigger. - Decisive decision or feedback right: develop/revise strategic options and recommend changes when environmental assumptions or future scenarios alter the viable choice set. - Decision owner: the autonomous Hermes actor applying Corporate Strategy / Chief Strategy Officer reasoning. - A witness mode: the documented one-profile-for-the-whole-organization deployment, in which Strategy, Executive and Finance skills are reachable to the same autonomous actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scenario templates, assumption lists, early-warning indicators with ownership/review cadence, cross-skill routing, Executive escalation and Finance allocation authority. - Closure path: external/future distinction → model-driven strategic option/recommendation → Executive/Finance decision context → present commitment, capital, budget or other capability consequence inside the same organization-wide agent loop. Scenario planning also requires an action to take now and an observable trigger for revisiting the plan. - Boundary reachability: the installed single Executive profile resolves corporate-strategy, scenario-planning and Finance/Executive skills in the same Henterprise tree; prospective judgment can return into that actor&#39;s current decision context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the prospective judgment and option generation are model-driven, and the documented single-profile mode supplies a first-party return path into the same actor&#39;s Executive/Finance current-control context. The Profile does not require a background monitoring daemon; indicator ownership/cadence and trigger-based revisit are supporting evidence rather than a prerequisite for a separate process. - Evidence: top-level README.md; corporate-strategy/chief-strategy-officer/SKILL.md; corporate-strategy/scenario-planning/SKILL.md; and finance/chief-financial-officer/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the migration omits Headcount&#39;s stronger statement that strategy is unfinished until specific resourcing artifacts visibly move, and the alternative multi-profile deployment does not itself propagate strategy state across profiles. Neither gap defeats the closed single-profile witness mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A - Function: establish organizational direction, identity boundaries, and ultimate arbitration where lower-level functions cannot settle a question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: disagreement over what the organization is for, what it will not do, which strategy is authoritative, and cross-functional conflicts that require an organization-level rather than departmental choice. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: organizational purpose/direction, explicit exclusions, strategy of record, priority stack, and final policy-level cross-functional trade-offs. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the autonomous Executive Hermes actor in the supplied organization-wide single-profile mode, bounded by its documented role; ordinary operator setup does not create a separate parent-owned S5 mode. - Return-to-operation path: the Executive policy/priority decision is retained as the current direction and handed back into subordinate work as the active governing constraint within the same supported full-tree profile. - Decisive decision or feedback right: set direction, own the strategy of record and priority stack, and resolve ultimate internal policy conflicts no subordinate executive can settle. - Decision owner: the autonomous Hermes actor operating under the supplied Executive persona / Chief Executive role. - A witness mode: the documented one-profile-for-the-whole-organization deployment, for which Henterprise explicitly identifies executive/SOUL.md as the natural organization-wide soul. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Executive SOUL.md, chief-executive skill, subordinate escalation rules, strategy/priority artifacts, and the six-part decision/return contract. - Closure path: identity/policy conflict → model-driven Chief Executive decision → explicit priority/strategy/handoff consequences with owners and dates → subsequent work in the same organization-wide agent context is governed by that decision. - Boundary reachability: the shipped executive/SOUL.md and Chief Executive skills are explicitly selected for the full-tree Hermes profile, where the agent&#39;s identity/policy judgment can constrain subsequent work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Henterprise gives the Executive role actual direction/strategy/final-tradeoff decision rights rather than merely static policy text, and the supported single-profile mode closes the return into subsequent operation without requiring a developer-composed cross-profile control plane. - Evidence: top-level README.md; executive/SOUL.md; executive/chief-executive/SKILL.md; and corporate-strategy/chief-strategy-officer/SKILL.md for final strategy ownership. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the Executive persona says genuinely owner-reserved matters should be returned to the owner, but the repository does not define a sufficiently specific identity/ultimate-policy parent trigger and returned decision loop to publish A(P). Generic human installation, invocation, or override is not enough.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/db04f834fa3448a971185a5fc30cfe0ba0fb9516/assessments/henterprise.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit db04f834fa3448a971185a5fc30cfe0ba0fb9516\">db04f83 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"6\" data-a=\"6\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T18:20:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/razzant/ouroboros\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ouroboros</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"6\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>6</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/razzant/ouroboros/tree/86806ee123ce8e26cc063cc1a618f975eea64f26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 86806ee123ce8e26cc063cc1a618f975eea64f26\">86806ee &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform general-purpose task work through a first-party persistent model/tool decision-action loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous goals, changing project/environment state, tool observations/failures, specialist results and uncertainty about the next action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive tools/actions, interpret observations, delegate when useful, revise approach and decide when a deliverable is ready. - Decision owner: the active Ouroboros Main/worker model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OuroborosAgent, tool registry/execution, durable context/memory, Supervisor queue, task contracts, budgets, guards and result/artifact persistence. - Closure path: task + durable context → model decision → tool/action → observation → next model decision → terminal outcome. - Boundary reachability: ordinary local direct/managed operation enters the shipped Ouroboros loop directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code transports/enforces actions but the model chooses substantive action from current evidence. - Evidence: docs/architecture/06-agent-core.md, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model/coding-harness providers are inference/execution substrates; their metasystem functions are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among parallel specialist contributions before they enter the parent body/result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently produced patches can overlap, conflict, represent mutually exclusive alternatives or become unsafe after target drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: compare child results/patches and choose one, synthesize several or reject/defer a contribution. - Decision owner: the parent Ouroboros model/Main. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: private snapshots, patch custody, manifests/hashes, baseline-drift checks, Git staging, protected-path gates and disposition records. - Closure path: parallel child work → isolated result/patch candidates → Main integration judgment → host safely applies/verifies chosen disposition → later parent work sees coordinated state. - Boundary reachability: standard subagent/result/compare/integration tools expose the complete parent coordination path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: isolation/drift checks are enforcement; the parent model owns the discretionary choice among competing contributions. - Evidence: ouroboros/tools/subagentintegration.py, docs/architecture/06-agent-core.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the removed semantic duplicate-task gate is deliberately not used as S2 evidence. - Distinct S1 units: native/delegated specialist agents performing independent operational work for the same parent/task organization. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their independently authored contributions can overlap or conflict when returned to the same parent worktree/body. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent receives isolated candidates and explicitly compares/accepts/synthesizes/rejects before integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the selected disposition changes the parent body/result that subsequent Main and child work operate on. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path specifically regulates interference among concurrent contributions at the integration boundary; generic delegation/message transport is not the basis of the claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments/allocation across active root tasks/projects and their specialist task trees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple active/pending roots, project bindings, specialist work, new owner messages, child completion/custody and cancellation/intervention needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect current roots/projects and choose steering/routing/promotion, specialist creation, cancellation and child disposition/integration. - Decision owner: the active Main decision turn. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mainroutingmanifest, current-chat task facts, Supervisor queue/state, routing receipts, cancellation custody, worker caps and project registry. - Closure path: current whole-runtime facts + new demand/result → Main current-control judgment → routing/delegation/cancel/integration action → changed commitments/state → refreshed facts. - Boundary reachability: standard context builder supplies current manifests to Main and standard control tools expose intervention paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervisor enforces queue/custody correctness but does not choose which work to steer, reroute or augment; Main does. - Evidence: ouroboros/serverroutingcontext.py, ouroboros/tools/control.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: owner task-card Stop/steering is not promoted to (P) without a stronger distinct parent whole-system current-control topology. - Whole-system current view: mainroutingmanifest/current-chat context exposes addressable running/pending roots, projects and relevant result/work-location facts. - Current-control decision scope: continue versus reroute/promote work, specialist allocation, child disposition/integration and applicable cancellation/intervention.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect operational/self-modification results and return defects into corrective Main/author operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: hidden defects, scope mistakes, constitutional/quality violations and objective failures missed by the producer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate reviewer actors inspect a bound candidate/evidence and issue independent findings/verdicts that can require correction/fresh review. - Decision owner: configured independent reviewer actors for audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer routing, separate review executors, exact-subject binding, read-only/retrieval evidence, task-acceptance panels, commit gates and persisted verdicts. - Closure path: candidate → independent review → findings/verdict → author/Main correction/rebuttal → revised candidate/fresh review or explicitly governed continuation. - Boundary reachability: independent task/commit review is shipped first-party behavior in supported standard modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit judgment is produced by separate model identities rather than deterministic checks or the producer simply rereading itself. - Evidence: BIBLE.md, docs/architecture/06-agent-core.md, ouroboros/reviewexecution.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Cyber Pro can make review non-vetoing; that changes enforcement authority, not the existence/independence of the reviewer path. - Claim being audited: the task result or proposed self-modification is correct, complete, in scope and acceptable under the applicable contract/checklists. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing Main/worker&#39;s own trajectory, tool results, tests and candidate output. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer models receive the bound candidate plus independent/retrieval evidence and applicable criteria. - Independence boundary: reviewer execution/identity is distinct from the producing Main/worker; exact subject/evidence binding prevents the producer from silently substituting another candidate. - Who acts on findings: Main/author receives findings and performs correction/rebuttal before fresh review or an explicitly governed continuation decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: turn completed-work experience and accumulated capability evidence into reviewed changes to Ouroboros&#39;s future code/process capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurrent failure classes, bottlenecks/missing capabilities, improvement backlog and history of absorbed/abandoned/no-op evolution objectives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — Main decides whether/what self-improvement to promote; parent mode — owner can explicitly start an evolution campaign with a chosen objective and retains sticky stop authority. - Decision owner: base mode — Ouroboros Main model; parent mode — human owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-task reflection, Improvement Backlog, solve-capability history, durable promotion request, campaign transactions, independent review/commit/promotion/restart gates. - Closure path: completed-work evidence → reflection/backlog/history → objective judgment → evolution campaign → reviewed change → promotion/restart → changed capability for later operation. - Boundary reachability: post-task evolution is wired into root processing and Supervisor consumption; owner/agent evolution controls use the same shipped campaign lifecycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: host code gates cadence/safety, while the base mode&#39;s promote/objective decision is explicitly a Main-slot LLM judgment. - Evidence: ouroboros/posttaskevolution.py, supervisor/evolutionlifecycle.py, supervisor/eventsruntimecontrols.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: autonomous post-task evolution is default-off and can be owner-stopped; A describes the supported enabled autonomous mode. - External distinction: real completed-task reflections, observed failure/bottleneck patterns and accumulated improvement/capability history. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a discovered limitation is worth changing Ouroboros&#39;s future solve capability rather than only finishing the present task. - Adaptation option generated: a concrete self-improvement objective selected from reflection/backlog/history, carrying plan-review obligations where applicable. - Path back into current capability / S3: reviewed campaign changes are promoted/restarted into the live Ouroboros body, altering capabilities available to later operational/current-control work.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: determine/maintain Ouroboros&#39;s identity/constitutional direction and ultimate internal policy for cognition, review and self-modification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts among agency, continuity, review/integrity and self-creation; proposed constitutional/identity changes; decisions about review authority, cognitive horizon and protected rewrite posture. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Cyber Pro allows Ouroboros to choose its own review/internal-policy/configuration posture and protected rewrites; ordinary modes retain explicit owner authority over specified ultimate-policy surfaces. - Decision owner: base mode — Ouroboros in Cyber Pro; parent mode — human owner outside Cyber Pro for owner-reserved policy decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BIBLE.md, identity.md, always-loaded identity context, runtime-mode policy, owner settings persistence, protected-path/review machinery and owner-ingress provenance. - Closure path: identity/ultimate-policy matter → legitimate authority for selected mode → constitutional/identity/settings judgment → persisted runtime/context → later cognition/self-modification governed by the returned decision. - Boundary reachability: Constitution/identity are live first-party runtime inputs/mutation targets and shipped runtime modes materially change who owns policy/protected-rewrite authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: file-writing alone is not the basis; the decisive evidence is the explicit Cyber Pro transfer of internal governance/configuration judgment to Ouroboros, alongside a distinct owner-governed ordinary mode. - Evidence: BIBLE.md, ouroboros/runtimemodepolicy.py, web/modules/settingsui.js, docs/architecture/07-configuration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: selecting/saving Cyber Pro remains a human-controlled mode transition in the shipped UI; this does not change who owns policy judgments once that supported mode is active. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Ouroboros explicitly treats BIBLE.md as Constitution and identity.md as self-understanding, with review/horizon/self-creation rules defining who it is and how its agency may govern itself. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Cyber Pro assigns internal policy/configuration judgment to Ouroboros; ordinary reviewed modes reserve specified review/context/ultimate-policy decisions to the owner. - Return-to-operation path: constitutional/identity/settings changes are persisted and loaded into later runtime context/policy enforcement, changing subsequent S1/S3/S4 operation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/861643ffba6b504c415bfa0f3e8b2d366f30ea4e/assessments/ouroboros.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 861643ffba6b504c415bfa0f3e8b2d366f30ea4e\">861643f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-23T20:42:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cbrock84/headcount\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Headcount</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cbrock84/headcount/tree/9cbf34005e3e8a980a6af9b55eb226bd926a62b3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9cbf34005e3e8a980a6af9b55eb226bd926a62b3\">9cbf340 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: department specialist agents perform the organization&#39;s primary transformations by producing analyses, plans, reviews, implementations and other domain outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: domain-specific uncertainty across finance, product, technology, security, legal, operations, revenue, people and other local environments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the domain reasoning/action needed to produce the requested bounded outcome within the department skill/remit. - Decision owner: the model-driven department specialist instantiated through the first-party Headcount plugin/skill path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: independently installable department plugins, skill auto-loading/invocation and host model/tool execution. - Closure path: the specialist produces the domain artifact/decision/review under its return contract and returns it into the invoking organization/orchestrator or directly into the target work surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the decisive domain judgment is made by the autonomous specialist actor; Headcount&#39;s skill machinery defines remit and constraints but does not substitute a deterministic answer. - Evidence: README.md and docs/GETTING-STARTED.md document installed department specialists and direct skill engagement; docs/USE-CASES.md shows departments producing concrete business/project outcomes. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate interference among multiple operational agents when work can be parallelized safely and serialize it when dependencies or shared write surfaces would make parallel work destructive. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two agents editing the same file, sequential dependencies, incompatible parallel work and merge/lost-update conflicts between otherwise autonomous producers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the current work can be split, define exclusive agent surfaces/briefs and choose parallel versus sequential execution/integration. - Decision owner: the autonomous main/orchestrator agent applying Headcount&#39;s shipped parallel-agent-delivery method. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the first-party disjoint-surface/dependency rules, structured returns and optional agent-hierarchy guard pattern when the user composes a persistent roster. - Closure path: the orchestrator partitions only disjoint independent work for parallel dispatch; unsafe work remains sequential; returned results are reviewed/reconciled before integration and later work observes the integrated state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent decides decomposition and whether parallelism is valid from actual work dependencies/surfaces; deterministic guard machinery, when adopted, only verifies the selected partition. - Evidence: parallel-agent-delivery explicitly identifies same-file concurrent writes as merge-conflict/lost-update failures and instructs the agent to run sequentially unless disjointness/dependency/verification conditions hold; agent-hierarchy supplies a persistent form of the same surface discipline. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic cross-department sequencing and the maintainer&#39;s root surface map are not the positive witness; the mapping rests on the shipped agent-owned inter-agent write/dependency coordination method. - Distinct S1 units: two or more department/builder agents operating on separately assigned work surfaces or domain outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes can create merge conflicts or silent lost updates, while sequential dependencies can make premature parallel execution invalid. - Attenuating coordination relation: the orchestrator establishes disjoint write surfaces and dependency independence before dispatch, otherwise keeps work sequential. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the chosen partition determines which agents run, their allowed surfaces and when dependent work may begin; failed coordination conditions prevent conflicting parallel execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Headcount names a concrete destructive interaction between S1s and supplies an agent-owned coordination decision specifically to attenuate it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current organization-wide priorities, capital/attention allocation, cross-functional commitments and conflicts on behalf of the whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: specialist-local optimization, mutually incompatible department recommendations, excessive simultaneous priorities and current resource/attention conflicts that cannot be settled within one function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which cross-functional consideration wins, what is not pursued, where capital/attention goes and which current priority becomes binding. - Decision owner: the autonomous Chief Executive agent role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cross-department return contracts, priority stack, strategy/decision artifacts, escalation conventions and orchestrator delegation. - Closure path: functional specialists return their positions; the Chief Executive makes the cross-functional choice and issues explicit handoffs/priority/allocation decisions that govern subsequent department work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Chief Executive skill explicitly states that one agent, rather than the orchestrator or specialist, owns the call when specialists disagree; deterministic routing is not being credited as S3. - Evidence: chief-executive/SKILL.md assigns capital/attention allocation, cross-functional arbitration and final say on tradeoffs to the agent and requires concrete handoffs in the return contract; docs/USE-CASES.md routes multi-function situations to executive closure where needed. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: owner-reserved matters can still be deferred to the human; no separate parent S3 mode is published because the pinned evidence does not reconstruct a distinct standard current-control parent loop with the specificity required for A(P). - Whole-system current view: the Chief Executive receives cross-functional analyses, constraints, costs, risks and competing current priorities from the relevant departments. - Current-control decision scope: current priority stack, capital/attention allocation, cross-functional tradeoffs, present-quarter conflicts and handoffs across functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge producer/department claims through a separate reviewer that inspects the underlying artifact/evidence and can return blocking or corrective findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic producer self-report, unsafe design/implementation, legal/security facts hidden by ordinary functional summaries and self-review bias. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently judge the producer&#39;s artifact/finding and issue a blocking or pass/corrective review outcome. - Decision owner: constructor path in the assessed installable-plugin mode. Headcount supplies the review method and independence contract, but /plugin install security@headcount at the pinned revision does not itself deliver a distinct autonomous reviewer actor that owns this judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer-class skill semantics, agent-hierarchy producer/auditor separation, read-only reviewer requirements, blocking-findings return contracts and optional guard machinery when a user instantiates the hierarchy. - Closure path: when composed in the assessed installable-plugin mode, a separate reviewer inspects the actual artifact/evidence, returns findings and blocking status, and the orchestrator/Chief Executive plus responsible producer consume the result before further commitment. The first-party method defines this loop, but the independent reviewer identity/wiring must still be instantiated in that mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-hierarchy specifically exposes the organizational audit primitive and requires producer and auditor to be different, while security-architecture-review defines the complementary raw-artifact judgment and blocking return. Separately, root .claude/agents/security-review.md already demonstrates a concrete autonomous reviewer in Headcount&#39;s project-local repository mode. That is positive evidence that the actor design is real; it does not upgrade this assessment to A because the published system boundary is the independently installed department-plugin mode, where the security plugin at the pinned revision ships the review skill but not that distinct reviewer actor. - Evidence: agent-hierarchy/SKILL.md requires producer/auditor separation and read-only reviewers; docs/GETTING-STARTED.md and docs/USE-CASES.md describe reviewer-class blocking semantics; security-architecture-review/SKILL.md requires reading the actual artifact and returning blocking findings/fixes; plugins/security/.claude-plugin/plugin.json defines the installed security-plugin boundary; root docs/AGENT-SURFACES.md / .claude/agents/security-review.md demonstrate an autonomous project-local reviewer implementation that is not packaged by the security plugin at the pinned revision. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: this C is mode-relative, not a claim that Headcount lacked any autonomous reviewer implementation. The repository-root/project-local Claude Code mode already contains such an actor; this assessment does not publish a separate vector for that mode. A supported installable distribution that packages/wires the independent reviewer could justify A without changing the S3 functional mapping. - Claim being audited: that a producer&#39;s design/change/functional recommendation is safe, legally/technically acceptable or ready to proceed. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing department/specialist returns its own artifact and summary through its normal engagement/return contract. - Complementary access path: the supplied review skills/method require a separate reviewer to inspect the actual design/change/artifact and applicable evidence rather than relying on the producer&#39;s self-assessment. - Independence boundary: independence is an explicit first-party construction rule. The root project-local mode demonstrates a distinct reviewer actor concretely; in the assessed installable-plugin mode, that actor must still be instantiated by the host/user organization at the pinned revision. - Who acts on findings: the orchestrator/Chief Executive and responsible producer consume the blocking finding, correction and escalation path once the reviewer lane has been composed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model external/future conditions, develop strategic adaptation options and return them into present resource/capability decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing markets, competitors, partnerships, acquisition opportunities, uncertain multi-year conditions and early signs that strategic assumptions may be becoming false. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the strategic interpretation/options/recommendation and the indicators that should trigger revision of the plan. - Decision owner: the autonomous Chief Strategy Officer agent role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scenario framing, competitive analysis, assumptions/early-warning indicators, portfolio analysis, strategy artifacts and CEO/CFO handoff. - Closure path: the CSO develops options/recommendation from external/future evidence and hands the conclusion to the Chief Executive/Finance; the strategy is not considered finished until resourcing artifacts such as hiring/roadmap/incentives move, closing the S4→S3 conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-driven CSO owns the prospective strategic judgment; the CEO retains present allocation/final strategy-of-record authority, which is the required S4/S3 relationship rather than evidence against S4 autonomy. - Evidence: chief-strategy-officer/SKILL.md assigns markets, partnerships, acquisitions, multi-year planning, scenarios and early-warning indicators to the role; it explicitly instructs the CSO to hand conclusions to CEO/CFO and tests whether resourcing artifacts actually changed. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic planning/backlog work is not credited; the positive witness is the explicit external and multi-year strategic role with a return into present allocation. - External distinction: market/segment choice, competitor investment/pricing behavior, strategic partnerships, acquisitions/divestitures and other changes outside current internal execution. - Future / prospective distinction: multi-year horizon, scenarios, early-warning indicators and falsifiable assumptions about what could change the strategy. - Adaptation option generated: where to play, build/buy/partner/exit choices, portfolio funding recommendations and strategic tradeoffs. - Path back into current capability / S3: recommendation goes to Chief Executive/Finance and is only operationally finished when headcount/roadmap/incentive/resource artifacts move.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A - Function: maintain organizational direction, identity and ultimate internal policy closure when cross-functional present/future tensions require a legitimate final organizational decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguity about what the organization is for, what it will not do, which strategy is authoritative and which cross-functional tradeoff should define the organization rather than one department. - Decisive decision or feedback right: set organizational direction, decide what will not be pursued, own the strategy of record and close final internal cross-functional policy choices. - Decision owner: the autonomous Chief Executive agent role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: strategy of record, priority stack, explicit executive authority/remit, department escalation paths and six-part return/handoff contract. - Closure path: unresolved cross-functional or strategic matters reach the Chief Executive; the agent makes the direction/policy decision and returns explicit priority/handoff consequences that govern later department work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: chief-executive/SKILL.md explicitly gives the role ownership of direction and final internal cross-functional calls rather than merely displaying a static policy prompt; human-owner-reserved decisions remain a caveat and are not silently promoted to a parent mode without a separately evidenced closure protocol. - Evidence: the Chief Executive remit includes “what the organization is for, and what it will not do,” owns the strategy of record/priority stack/final tradeoffs and is the escalation endpoint for organizational decisions; the CSO explicitly leaves final strategy approval/ownership with this role. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: the skill states that genuinely owner-reserved decisions should be identified rather than decided for the human. The reviewed standard distribution does not specify a sufficiently explicit separate parent S5 trigger/return protocol to publish A(P), so only the evidenced agent-owned internal S5 closure is encoded. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: organizational purpose/direction, authoritative strategy and final policy-level cross-functional choice. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Chief Executive agent for the claimed autonomous internal mode. - Return-to-operation path: the Chief Executive&#39;s strategy/priority/tradeoff decision produces explicit handoffs and changes the direction/allocation under which departments subsequently operate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/928b402ecac8dd969382fb0d9b0b461b89e9290f/assessments/headcount.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 928b402ecac8dd969382fb0d9b0b461b89e9290f\">928b402 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T02:10:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/r1c7/CluxMate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CluxMate</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/r1c7/CluxMate/tree/c3d5d84bbb6149ebdd2bd0ec7a8fa7817a984192\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c3d5d84bbb6149ebdd2bd0ec7a8fa7817a984192\">c3d5d84 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding, inspection, file modification and verification requests; delegated children independently pursue bounded subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing codebase requirements, missing context, tool errors, differing file structure and failed checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select an action/tool, evaluate the result, change the next action and decide when to complete within the task boundary. - Decision owner: model-driven parent AgentLoop, and model child loops over delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool bridge, sandbox/fences, permissions, LSP, shell, checkpoint snapshotting and event log. - Closure path: user task → model decision → approved tool/child action → result/log and diagnostics → next model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: AgentBuilder registers task/file/shell/LSP tools for the default live agent and TaskTool constructs executable children; CLI/TUI/desktop all run this core. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects the concrete work, tool call and response to feedback; the user chooses the objective and risk ceiling and the host enforces safety. - Evidence: agent loop, builder tool registration, task execution. - Basis: source-level standard-distribution closure, not a live agent run. - Confidence: high on mechanism/ownership; runtime effectiveness unmeasured. - Caveats: no model/provider-quality benchmark run; dangerous work can require human approval.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: coordinate distinct delegated coding S1 units so simultaneous work does not race on the same workspace paths or exhaust shared execution slots. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child write/write conflict, uncontrolled fan-out, a nested child waiting for its parent&#39;s saturated slot. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model parent chooses task partition and the writepaths claims on each task call; when a child cannot be admitted or reports completion the parent receives its outcome and can replan. The deterministic scheduler enforces the selected partition and structural overlap rules. - Decision owner: model parent for discretionary partition and coordination; SubagentScheduler for fixed admission enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXCONCURRENT=4, MAXWRITERS=2, path canonicalization, overlap detection, writer serialization, nested fail-fast and ticket release. - Closure path: parent allocates tasks/scopes → scheduler compares active claims → executes disjoint children, queues conflicting top-level writers or refuses blocked nested spawns → completed/blocked child status returns in the parent task result → parent changes downstream assignment. - Boundary reachability: shipped parent TaskTool exposes writepaths and calls SubagentScheduler.acquire before child construction; the scheduler releases slots under finally. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects work boundaries, coordinating scope and subsequent plan; the host&#39;s fixed cap and serialization alone would warrant only a coordination constructor, not model ownership. Positive A is bounded to parent-mediated child partition, not an independent dynamic negotiation protocol among all children. - Distinct S1 units: two or more delegated general-purpose writer children, with optional explore and reviewer children. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writes to equal, ancestor or descendant workspace paths and competition for shared execution capacity. - Attenuating coordination relation: declared claim scopes, overlap admission gate, deferred incompatible writers and limited model-available parallel slots. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: scheduler denial/queuing affects what can execute now, and structured child status/failed nested spawn returns to the model parent for a changed assignment or continuation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it explicitly detects and attenuates inter-child write conflicts, with a model-selected scope boundary, rather than merely offering a mailbox, task list or child-spawn primitive. - Evidence: scheduler, task write claims / feedback, scheduler tests. - Basis: explicit end-to-end runtime wiring plus host tests; no LLM test. - Confidence: medium-high; coordination discretion is model-mediated, fixed admission decisions remain runtime-owned. - Caveats: omitted paths conservatively claim the whole workspace; uncontrolled parent direct writes are not proven serializable against children; guarantees are about declared child claims and scheduling, not universal data-race freedom.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: integrate concurrent work, set current task commitments and resource/priority trade-offs, act on child/audit evidence, and steer execution of the session whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task fragmentation, incomplete/false successes, bounded child budgets, current permission constraints, competing work priorities and execution failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model parent decomposes and allocates ongoing work and accepts/corrects child work; alternatively the supervising user steers current commitments, mode and recovery after observing workspace/session status. - Decision owner: agent parent in autonomous delegated mode; human operator in the supported attended session mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: root-only todo, child tree/turn logs, child-status normalization, tool approval, session checkpoint/rewind, desktop agent inspector and session controls. - Closure path: current task and child reports/diffs → parent model&#39;s priority/correct/retry/review decision → additional tool and child actions; in attended mode user observes current task/session state → changes priorities/mode, interrupts or restores → next session operation follows that decision. - Boundary reachability: shipped root AgentLoop, TaskTool, parent-only TodoTool and UI/JSON-RPC current-session controls; operator intervention exists at the same workspace/session recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides the whole-task mix and reacts to returned children rather than the scheduler deciding organizational commitments; the user has a separate supported whole-session intervention route, not merely one write approval. - Whole-system current view: root task/todo, child execution tree and per-child status/log, current workspace diff, mode and checkpoint timeline in the local client. - Current-control decision scope: decompose and retask children, prioritize repair/review/completion, request further work, abort/rewind/restrict an ongoing session.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: separately inspect the gap between claimed requirement completion and directly observable code/test reality; return discrepancies for current-control correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementer self-report bias, omitted requirements, unsupported green statuses and regressions invisible in the ordinary child summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a distinct model reviewer judges each requirement claim on source/test evidence and returns pass/fail/unverifiable; the parent model can demand correction and a bounded scoped re-review. - Decision owner: first-party spawned reviewer model for independent audit judgment; parent model S3 receives findings and changes work priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in reviewer profile has no edit tools; separate child session and prompt, raw read/grep/LSP/bash checks, no implementer-summary input, path-claim serialization, normalized machine-readable child status, narrower host claim-audit reminder. - Closure path: implementation artifact and original requirement → distinct reviewer inspects raw tree, executes allowed deterministic check → per-claim findings returned by task → root model fixes failures → optional single scoped re-review before completion. - Boundary reachability: reviewer is a built-in standard type and the root system prompt explicitly dispatches it after nontrivial implementation work, with original requirements and diff; child builder builds its own restricted loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer model owns actual discrepancy judgment, distinct from implementer&#39;s self-report; scheduler and advisory completion audit only support the independent path. Parent controls review triggering, so it is not mandatory for every run. - Claim being audited: whether nontrivial delivered code satisfies each requested requirement and whether a child&#39;s reported success is backed by evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer child → completion narrative/status → parent model and task/turn events. - Complementary access path: reviewer gets requirement plus file/diff reference, reads actual workspace and can run verification commands; excludes implementer narrative. - Independence boundary: separate child context, restricted toolset and actual code/test evidence bypass implementer summary; same configured provider/model family, reviewer dispatch owner and mutable workspace remain possible correlated failure points. - Who acts on findings: model parent S3 fixes/retasks and invokes at most one targeted re-review; a human may inspect the report in attended mode. - Evidence: reviewer registry and evidentiary contract, root review gate instructions, child status reconciliation, review gate tests. - Basis: specific independent auditor actor, direct operational evidence and explicit corrective return path in standard prompts, not CI alone. - Confidence: medium-high on shipped path, medium on real-world independence. - Caveats: text/prompt obligations are not host-enforced audit coverage; a reviewer with bash can technically alter files subject to instruction/permissions; no experiment measures capture resistance or verdict accuracy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: take durable lessons about changing project/environment constraints and use them to adapt future coding behavior across sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: rediscovery of non-obvious conventions, previously encountered build/test gotchas, and lost project-specific experience as future tasks differ. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root model is explicitly instructed to decide when a non-obvious, reusable learning merits writing to durable memory and what guidance should persist; the next turn&#39;s injected memory changes its available response policy/context. - Decision owner: root model agent selecting and recording prospective lessons; host stores and reinjects them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updatememory writes project/global AGENTS.md, always-injected future project memory; optional opt-in remember/forget plus lexical recall of stable facts. - Closure path: emerging project requirement/tool failure/environment observation → model determines reusable future distinction and formulates memory entry → durable project/global AGENTS.md or optional fact store → later request reinjects or recalls the lesson → model adjusts approach. - Boundary reachability: root AgentBuilder includes UpdateMemoryTool in regular non-plan modes; root system prompt explicitly calls for proactive learned context; memory manager reads on subsequent requests. Retrieval memory is optional and not needed for the base loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model chooses to distill and persist the adaptation signal; this is bounded knowledge/capability adaptation, not a claim of autonomous architecture redevelopment, systematic outside-market surveillance or policy authorship. A user can also edit memory, but a separate human-owned strategic S4 cycle is not established merely from editable instructions. - External distinction: new, non-obvious repo/environment conventions, build/test behavior and constraints experienced while carrying out coding tasks. - Future / prospective distinction: whether those lessons will recur and ought to change subsequent sessions, versus transient details to discard. - Adaptation option generated: model-authored durable memory instruction/fact, with potential later correction or removal. - Path back into current capability / S3: MemoryManager.render and builder.injectionsforturn inject the revised future-session memory for current model task planning and execution. - Evidence: proactive learning prompt, update-memory tool, memory readback, root tool registration, optional retrieval memory. - Basis: standard root-prompt instruction plus write→persist→future-injection code path; no real model learning uplift measured. - Confidence: medium; prospective memory closure established, strategic breadth explicitly narrow. - Caveats: the agent need not record every useful lesson; disabled retrieval memory does not undermine always-injected AGENTS.md; user review/approval can be required for writes depending on mode; memory may be stale or truncated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain the workspace assistant&#39;s ultimate operational identity, trusted project boundary, allowed capabilities and risk-policy envelope under legitimate operator control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: request for unsafe tooling, untrusted project-provided instructions, escalation of dangerous shell code and drift of persistent authorization policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user determines authorized project trust, persistent safe/dangerous allowlists, sandbox grants, model configuration and allowed operating mode; critical/escalated calls retain human gating even when routine activity is autonomous. - Decision owner: local human/operator for the durable identity and ultimate-policy boundary; not the agent, provider or repository maintainer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionPolicy and TrustStore persistence, WriteFence, OS sandbox and SSRF/egress rules, scoped user approvals and mode switch. - Closure path: identity/authority question (trusted workspace, permitted risk tier, escalation/egress/sandbox ceiling) → operator approves/denies/configures → trust/permissions/config stored in protected user/project locations → effective toolset and next execution governed by selected policy. - Boundary reachability: supported user-global and per-project policy/config files, live prompt cards, CLI trust commands and desktop settings; model cannot simply write protected .cluxmate authority configuration through normal file tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent chooses task actions inside the operator&#39;s policy, but does not own the legitimate decision to change that ultimate envelope. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whom the agent may act for, which workspace it may trust and which risks remain forbidden or require explicit authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: human project/session owner; no autonomous first-party S5 policy-substitution mode established. - Return-to-operation path: persisted per-project/global decision is reloaded/consulted on later requests, changing available tools, approvals and enforcement. - Evidence: permission mode and persisted policy, trust registry, README security, builder sandbox enforcement. - Basis: explicit first-party durable user authority and enforcement return loop. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: yolo opt-out weakens the sandbox by intentional operator choice; this does not transfer ultimate authority to the model.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/45c257a2b5814205a1a87a0ca117d3576a2d325a/assessments/cl/cluxmate.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 45c257a2b5814205a1a87a0ca117d3576a2d325a\">45c257a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-07T13:59:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CCherry07/pi-rs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pi-rs</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-07\">2026-10-07</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CCherry07/pi-rs/tree/efb6925e248ea801448fd09e60623d109852d3c9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision efb6925e248ea801448fd09e60623d109852d3c9\">efb6925 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended coding work through model-selected first-party tools and iterative reaction to source/tool/provider results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code/task requirements, repository state, tool/test failures, provider outputs, user input, and session recovery/continuation conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select coding actions/tools, interpret results, change approach, delegate bounded work, and decide when the requested operation is complete. - Decision owner: the root coding agent, with subordinate worker agents owning delegated operational tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pi-agent loop/tool scheduler, Coding tool plugins, durable session journal/queues, compaction/recovery, project trust/resource generation, provider adapters. - Closure path: user/task input → model decision → first-party tool action → persisted tool/result evidence → next model decision → further action or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: Coding product assembly and supported frontends instantiate the same first-party agent/session/tool runtime at the frozen revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic Rust code executes, persists and constrains actions, but the model chooses task-specific operations and reacts to returned evidence; removing model discretion leaves execution machinery rather than materially the same coding operation. - Evidence: README; crates/pi-agent; domains/coding. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external providers supply inference; project trust and OS permissions bound reachable actions but do not own the open-ended task decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among simultaneously viable child S1 units by assigning disjoint write ownership, separating independent from dependency-constrained work, and reintegrating reports/messages before dependent action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-filesystem write collisions, dependency races, duplicated/conflicting work, and premature dependent decisions while peer work remains unsettled. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose child task/scope, decide which children may run concurrently, message or wait on dependencies, and re-plan after reports/messages. - Decision owner: the parent/root model-backed agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: asynchronous spawnagent, FIFO concurrency queue, stable child ids, durable collaboration messages, automatic report rejoin, waitagent barriers/races, follow-up sessions, root depth/spawn limits. - Distinct S1 units: model-backed reusable isolated child sessions performing bounded coding/research/review tasks under the parent session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: child sessions share the current working directory/filesystem, so parallel editors can collide; dependent work can also consume stale/incomplete peer results if advanced concurrently. - Attenuating coordination relation: shipped parent guidance requires disjoint write ownership for parallel editors, launches only independent children together, uses waits/messages for dependencies, and re-plans from returned results. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: bounded child reports/messages rejoin the parent context; wait results and statuses change which child/follow-up/dependent work the parent launches or accepts next. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the collaboration rules explicitly regulate a concrete shared-workspace/dependency interference risk among distinct concurrently viable S1 units and alter whether/how those units may execute. - Closure path: parent partitions work → independent child S1s run concurrently under disjoint ownership / dependent paths wait → reports/messages/status return → parent re-plans and launches/continues dependent S1 work. - Boundary reachability: all six collaboration operations are model-callable tools registered by the bundled first-party subagents plugin; parent guidance is injected into eligible root sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic concurrency limits/queues/messages enforce transport, while the parent model owns the substantive partition, independence/dependency judgment and response to peer results. - Evidence: subagent parent guidance; tool.rs; runtime.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: concurrency capacity/FIFO ordering itself is deterministic support; S2 ownership rests on the parent agent&#39;s conflict/dependency decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-current collaboration-tree view and regulate active child commitments through observation, messaging, waits, follow-up work and targeted interruption. - Disturbance / variety regulated: queued/running/settled child work, blocked dependencies, stale plans, child failure, need for new guidance, and active turns that should be stopped/reused. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the descendant tree, wait on any/all selected children, send guidance, launch follow-up work into an existing child session, interrupt one active child turn, and re-plan the live portfolio of delegated commitments. - Decision owner: the parent/root model-backed agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents, waitagent, sendmessage, followuptask, interruptagent, durable child/session status, root concurrency/depth budgets, recovery/checkpoint machinery. - Closure path: parent reads current descendant-tree/status/message/result evidence → chooses current intervention (wait/message/follow-up/interrupt/new spawn/re-plan) → runtime applies it → updated state/result/message returns → parent makes the next control decision. - Boundary reachability: collaboration management tools are first-party and model-callable in the bundled subagent mode; asynchronous child sessions remain addressable by stable ids across turns/follow-ups. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime state/cancellation transport expose and enforce controls, but the parent model interprets whole-current evidence and chooses the substantive intervention. - Whole-system current view: listagents returns a read-only snapshot of the caller&#39;s descendant agent tree; wait/list results include child lifecycle state and messages/results while the parent also holds its own task/session context. - Current-control decision scope: launch versus wait, dependency barriers/races, targeted message/guidance, reuse through follow-up turns, targeted interruption, and re-planning of active delegated commitments. - Evidence: tool.rs; runtime.rs; lib.rs parent guidance. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hard concurrency/depth/spawn budgets are enforcement rather than the S3 owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review coding/diff/plan/PR claims through a dedicated restricted child role and return a merge/acceptance judgment to the parent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation defects, scope drift, unsupported assumptions, missing edge cases, inconsistency with requirements, and false confidence in the author&#39;s own account. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the named review target with direct repository evidence and emit an evidence-backed BLOCK, OK, or OK with notes judgment. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed reviewer child agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer-specific system prompt, read/grep/find/ls-only toolset, no nested subagents, no inherited skills, isolated child session, automatic bounded-report rejoin to parent. - Claim being audited: the focal implementation/plan/diff/PR claim that work is correct, appropriately bounded and acceptable. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementing/root agent&#39;s own coding/tool evidence and completion narrative. - Complementary access path: reviewer directly reads/searches the repository target under its own restricted role and must ground findings in code/tests/docs/requirements rather than trusting the parent&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: distinct child session/model context, reviewer-specific prompt, no write or shell tools, no nested subagents/skills, and explicit evidence-backed verdict format. - Who acts on findings: the parent/root agent receives the normal child report, retains final acceptance/orchestration authority, and can launch corrective/follow-up implementation before accepting completion. - Closure path: parent has implementation/claim → spawns reviewer → reviewer independently inspects direct evidence → verdict/finding automatically rejoins parent context → parent accepts, repairs or launches follow-up work. - Boundary reachability: reviewer is a built-in profile in the bundled subagent plugin and can be selected by the model-callable spawnagent path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment comes from a separate constrained model context with direct evidence access; deterministic role/tool restriction and result transport support rather than replace that judgment. - Evidence: reviewer.md; profiles.rs; tool.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reviewer use is a supported reachable mode rather than an unconditional gate on every coding turn.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert evidence from completed user/task experience into model-selected reusable future capability and maintain that capability under bounded ownership/provenance rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preferences/workflow corrections, newly verified techniques, stale or incomplete reusable procedures, overlapping managed skills, and future recurrence of task classes that would otherwise repeat avoidable discovery/error. - External distinction: the background review examines completed foreground conversation/tool experience and durable user/task signals as evidence distinct from the current session&#39;s frozen skill/memory capability state. - Future / prospective distinction: the skill-review policy explicitly asks what a future session would benefit from, requires class-level reusable procedures rather than one-off task narratives, and rejects unresolved failures as future guidance. - Adaptation option generated: patch a currently loaded managed skill, extend an existing umbrella, add reusable references/templates/scripts, create a new class-level skill when no umbrella fits, or (under Curator safeguards) consolidate/archive redundant agent-managed skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the background review model chooses whether supported learning warrants no-op, update, creation, or bounded consolidation and selects the content/procedure to persist for future reuse. - Decision owner: the model-backed Hermes background review / Curator review agent within its bounded managed-skill scope. - A witness mode: the default Hermes provider has reviewenabled: true; eligible user sessions periodically mark memory/skill review due, and after a final response the plugin can run a fresh background review with skillview/skillslist/skillmanage under review-private read-before-write observations. Agent-created managed skills become reusable Pi-native skills after catalog/resource reload. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review cadence counters, fresh review-run state, ownership/pin/content-hash/provenance checks, current-review read-before-write guard, trusted-project scope, archives/backups, Curator due/idle gate, session/resource reload. - Closure path: completed user/task experience → fresh background model review distinguishes durable future-relevant learning → model selects/edits/creates bounded agent-managed skill capability → guarded write persists it → later resource/skill catalog reload exposes the adapted procedure to subsequent pi-rs operation. - Path back into current capability / S3: the guarded skill write becomes part of the first-party managed skill library; on a later product/resource reload or subsequent session, that skill is projected into the active agent capability surface, where the root agent can use the adapted procedure in current coding/delegation decisions. Thus the prospective adaptation returns to the same pi-rs operational/control recursion rather than terminating as an external report. - Boundary reachability: Hermes is the built-in default memory provider and background review is enabled by default; the code registers the memory/skill tools and triggers review only for user-origin sessions under explicit cadence/final-response gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence and cadence are deterministic support, but the substantive future-relevance judgment, adaptation option and skill content are model-generated. Removing the review model leaves storage/guards but not materially the same adaptation decision. - Evidence: config.rs; lib.rs; skillreview.md; skillreview.rs; curator/runtime.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary MEMORY.md persistence alone would not establish S4; the positive claim relies on the separate model-backed future-reuse skill adaptation loop. Curator consolidation is optional, but the default-enabled background review already provides the A witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no model-backed authority to redefine pi-rs&#39;s ultimate identity, purpose, or governing principles is established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project trust, system prompts, plugin/resource generations, permission/OS boundaries, provider/model settings, pinned/user-owned skill protections. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project trust and protected-resource rules are strong governance/enforcement boundaries, but ultimate authority remains with user/developer configuration; the operating agents cannot authoritatively redefine those identity/policy boundaries. - Evidence: README project trust and resource sections; Hermes skill ownership safeguards. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: configurable policy, trust, protected skills and human authority do not become S5 merely because they constrain lower-level agents.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/53f5b1d912011f76fc5c99f41733e32a320866f0/assessments/pi-rs.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 53f5b1d912011f76fc5c99f41733e32a320866f0\">53f5b1d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T00:53:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/ArgusAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Argus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/ArgusAgent/tree/746f76b7a74a1217507c9ee348eecd3b782f7c92\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 746f76b7a74a1217507c9ee348eecd3b782f7c92\">746f76b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive Project work through model-driven engineering/research missions that inspect project state, choose implementation or experimental actions, execute tools and absorb returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository/workspace state, build/test failures, experimental outcomes, external data, tool results, implementation defects and domain-specific constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive operational actions inside an admitted mission and adapt subsequent actions from returned project/tool evidence. - Decision owner: the model-driven Engineer role; in Team mode each fresh teammate Engineer owns its bounded task-local operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planner mission brief, SkillLoop, tool/sandbox policy, vertical context, budgets, checkpoints and Reviewer feedback. - Closure path: mission admitted → Engineer inspects evidence and chooses work/tool actions → Argus executes and returns observations → Engineer adapts or reaches its decision point → result enters independent review/settlement. - Boundary reachability: Engineer and teammate Engineer missions are first-party standard runtime surfaces; external model backends supply inference but do not require downstream construction of the organizational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic operational choices are model-driven; host gates constrain execution and Reviewer controls acceptance without replacing Engineer task-local discretion. - Evidence: argusskill/loop.py; argusskill/team/teammateentry.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: stabilize parallel teammate S1 work inside one Agent Team by partitioning mutable work, respecting dependencies, preventing duplicate ownership/stage writes and returning independently settled shards to one synthesis path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate task claims; double-spawn into the same workdir after a live re-form; overlapping writable paths/shared mutable outputs; dependency-order violations; excessive concurrent width; and competing writes by multiple task-local teammates to shared Project stage state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether Team mode is warranted and choose the coordination arrangement—task decomposition, non-overlapping ownspaths, dependencies, priorities, pool width, and final synthesis—then revise/stop that arrangement when Team status or blocked work requires it. - Decision owner: the model-driven Engineer or explicitly assigned Team lead for semantic coordination choices. Curator owns deterministic process-lifecycle execution of the lead&#39;s admitted coordination plan but not the semantic partition/priority decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable task board; exclusive locks and atomic writes; compare-and-set claims; dependency-ready eligibility; bounded Curator pool; isolated teammate life roots/result shards; nested-team admission guard; holdsstageauthority=False; single-writer Curator lifecycle/leaderboard and lead synthesis. - Closure path: lead observes a parallelizable work package → chooses file-disjoint tasks/dependencies/width and forms the Team → Curator claims only eligible tasks and launches distinct teammate missions → runtime constraints prevent duplicate ownership and competing stage writes → teammate Reviewer-settled shards return to the shared board → lead inspects results, drains/dissolves the pool and writes one canonical synthesis → subsequent Project operation/review proceeds from that coordinated result. - Boundary reachability: Agent Team is a built-in Skill plus first-party argusskill.tools.team, daemon Curator, task board and teammate entrypoint reachable from a normal running Project; no downstream coordination subsystem must be invented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead&#39;s semantic partition/dependency/width/synthesis judgment while retaining locks/Curator would not make materially the same coordination decisions. Deterministic machinery enforces a chosen arrangement; it does not choose which tasks are safe to parallelize or how their outputs should be synthesized. - Evidence: argusskill/builtinskills/agent-team-lead.md; argusskill/tools/team.py; argusskill/team/taskboard.py; argusskill/team/teammateentry.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ownspaths is a lead coordination contract rather than a filesystem sandbox, so Team formation is explicitly withheld when prompt-level ownership is insufficient. That limitation is itself part of the S2 attenuation policy. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrently running fresh teammate Engineer→Reviewer missions, each with its own objective, acceptance evidence, local life state and result shard. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent teammates can otherwise claim the same task, touch the same mutable paths/output, violate dependency order, overrun admitted capacity, or independently write shared Project stage state from incompatible task-local views. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead-chosen disjoint task/path/dependency/priority/width plan, enforced by atomic task claims, dependency gating, one Curator process-lifecycle owner, isolated teammate state and suppression of teammate stage authority. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the durable board determines which teammate may run what next; failed/blocked/finished states change later claim eligibility and pool refill; result shards/leaderboard inform later teammate objectives and the lead&#39;s synthesis; draining/dissolution stops further teammate launches. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path is explicitly tied to concrete sibling interference in concurrent substantive operations and changes whether/how those S1 units may execute, own work and touch shared authority/state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain current control of the Project campaign by interpreting whole-Project evidence and deciding stage progression, rollback, hold or completion, with a distinct operator parent mode for live intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage mismatch, unresolved checklist/review obligations, failed or incomplete missions, objective changes, planner/reviewer conflicts, pauses and authorization needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode—decide/write current Project stage from present evidence; parent mode—pause, abort, steer, authorize or replace the standing objective. - Decision owner: autonomous mode—the model-driven Manager; parent mode—the operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign/control-head fingerprint, stage checklist, Reviewer/Planner evidence, strict decision parser, stage-machine gates and durable control state. - Closure path: current Project evidence assembled → Manager chooses advance/hold/rollback/complete → legal decision persists → later Planner/Engineer work follows it; alternatively operator control updates durable campaign state and later operation follows that decision. - Boundary reachability: Manager stage authority and operator control are wired into standard Project execution/daemon lifecycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Manager owns the semantic current-control choice; deterministic stage code validates/commits it. Parent controls are a separate supported mode. - Evidence: argusskill/manager/stageops.py; docs/FEATURES.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: deterministic completion gates and stale-context holds enforce but do not own the semantic S3 decision. - Whole-system current view: Manager binds current pipeline/campaign/control-head state, checklist and Reviewer/Planner evidence and may inspect Project evidence before deciding overall direction. - Current-control decision scope: Project stage advance/hold/rollback/complete, current workflow direction, standing-intent replacement and live pause/abort/authorization.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge Engineer claims/results against objective, direct artifacts, verification evidence and active vertical requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-reported success, implementation defects, regressions, weak evidence, incomplete scope and unsupported claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue done, continue, replanrequested or blocked from direct evidence and determine whether work settles or returns to Engineer/Planner/operator. - Decision owner: model-driven read-only Reviewer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer-specific role contract, read-only sandbox, artifact inspection, short checks, distinct role session/backend and host transition mapping. - Closure path: Engineer reports result → Reviewer directly inspects evidence → verdict → settlement, repair, replanning or operator escalation changes subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: independent review is first-party and wired into SkillLoop; teammate missions also retain their own Reviewer before returning shards. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model role makes the audit judgment and cannot edit the work it judges; host code transports/enforces the verdict. - Evidence: argusskill/loop.py; argusskill/roles/prompts/reviewer.py; argusskill/team/teammateentry.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: bounded low-risk paths may explicitly waive a separate Reviewer; the positive claim is for the standard independent-review mode. - Claim being audited: that the current Engineer or teammate result satisfies its admitted objective with adequate correctness/evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Engineer result, verification output, project changes and mission status. - Complementary access path: Reviewer directly opens project artifacts/results and may run short checks rather than trusting Engineer narrative. - Independence boundary: distinct read-only model role/session, unable to repair the artifact it judges. - Who acts on findings: Engineer repairs on continue, Planner changes direction on replanrequested, operator resolves parent-owned blockers, and Host settles accepted work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert evidence learned from completed Project/environment interaction into reviewed reusable procedures intended to improve later missions/sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring effective techniques, verified failure mechanisms, domain changes/patterns and role-review weaknesses not captured by current procedures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether settled evidence warrants a durable future-facing procedure and choose its content/scope. - Decision owner: isolated first-party model-driven TEAM learning reviewer; SELF learning supplies a parallel future-facing path for stable operator/conversation patterns. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-mission trigger, bounded candidate evidence, project→vertical→global scope rules, role Skill roots, reviewed receipts and quarantine constraints. - Closure path: mission settles with environment/project evidence → learning model judges durable lesson → reviewed Skill is created/updated when warranted → later role context loads it → later operation changes. - Boundary reachability: TEAM/SELF evolution is first-party runtime behavior invoked from the life supervisor; no downstream adaptation service is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime bounds evidence and timing, while a model makes the semantic adaptation choice and authors the retained procedure. - Evidence: argusskill/life/supervisor/evolution.py; argusskill/manager/skilltidy.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic memory/in-mission replanning is not credited; the A claim relies on explicit post-settlement later-session-oriented model review. - External distinction: settled work supplies distinctions about domain/project environment, effective techniques, failures and evidence limitations. - Future / prospective distinction: the learning path asks what reusable procedure should improve later work rather than only repair the current mission. - Adaptation option generated: create/update a scoped Manager/Planner/Engineer/Reviewer Skill or withhold an unsupported candidate. - Path back into current capability / S3: admitted role Skills are loaded into later role context and therefore affect subsequent operational/current-control decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: preserve operator-defined identity, red lines, standing purpose and reserved ultimate-policy decisions and return those choices into later Argus operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity/persona changes, standing-objective replacement, credentials/payment, irreversible actions, publication/external transmission and other questions autonomous roles are not legitimate to decide themselves. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define/edit persistent operator identity and purpose and decide actions explicitly reserved for human authority. - Decision owner: human operator as first-party parent authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent identity card, identity update surfaces, standing-objective lifecycle, Manager front door, durable parent controls and role contracts that stop at the authority boundary. - Closure path: identity/ultimate-policy matter reaches the operator → authoritative choice persists in first-party identity/control state → later role context or standing objective is updated → subsequent Manager/Planner/Engineer/Reviewer operation follows it. - Boundary reachability: identity, parent controls and standing-objective lifecycle ship with normal Argus operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Argus intentionally denies autonomous roles the legitimate right to rewrite operator-binding identity/red lines or self-authorize credentials, payment, irreversible actions or publication. - Evidence: README.md; argusskill/apps/initidentity.py; argusskill/life/memory.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary task approvals/stage transitions are not S5. No autonomous S5 mode is credited or implied. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: who Argus is for this operator, its red lines/standing purpose and whether actions outside delegated authority may proceed. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent mode only—the operator owns these rights. - Return-to-operation path: operator edits persist and are returned into later model context/campaign state; later operation runs under the authoritative decision.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c812b7dcf6f939d10b68cfb605919491e60d237f/assessments/argus-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c812b7dcf6f939d10b68cfb605919491e60d237f\">c812b7d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:48:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tigicion/dao-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dao Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tigicion/dao-code/tree/2b50e82c3de751c071e1610b39b3c95956853498\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b50e82c3de751c071e1610b39b3c95956853498\">2b50e82 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, edit, test and correct a user&#39;s software project through a model/tool/result loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, compile/test failure, unexpected command output, loss of context, blocked edits and changed user requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model selects code operations, tool arguments, next inspections, delegated subtasks and corrective attempts from observed responses. - Decision owner: model-backed dao parent or child agent; first-party loop dispatches and returns observations, while external model provider implements inference. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tools registry, approval gate, streaming API, session checkpoints, compaction, todo, shell processes and project instructions. - Closure path: user task and source context → model tool call → guarded execution → actual tool/test output → model repairs or concludes → changed project artifact/report. - Boundary reachability: the published dao CLI registers model-facing tools and runs the native turn loop; no external agent E2E or benchmark acceptance is needed to infer this first-party composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model decisions change next code/tool action in response to feedback; deterministic limits and permission gates do not supply that discretion. - Evidence: main model/tool loop, CLI runTurn wiring, first-party tools and loop description. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source path, medium for production inference quality. - Caveats: source/CLI architecture inspected at fixed ref; no external DeepSeek key, registered MCP provider or benchmark run was exercised.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate worktree/file-write interference among independent model-driven subagents by model-selected parallel versus serial assignments and explicit isolated branch partitioning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independently executing code-edit children might change the same project files or consume each other&#39;s partial uncommitted work. - Distinct S1 units: separate model/tool/result child agent runs that can inspect/edit code or tests and progress concurrently under tasks[] or background mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing writes/read-after-write assumptions to one shared git working tree create overwritten changes, invalid dependencies and ambiguous integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: parent model is instructed to parallelize only mutually independent tasks and decide when to use isolate/worktree to separate changes; chosen dispatch arguments create distinct git worktrees before child execution. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each child receives the selected isolated worktree as its effective project location; results and branch/change notices return to parent for subsequent review/integration or revised sequential dispatch. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this explicitly prevents a concrete collision between separately viable source-changing S1s, with the model selecting nonoverlap versus serial order and actual workspace partitioning, not merely handing messages to specialists. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses which subjobs can safely coexist and which need isolated workspaces or serial ordering, then issues the actual task and isolation arguments. - Decision owner: parent model in first-party agent tool protocol (A); worktree creation and MAXPARALLEL cap are constructor-owned support, not the agent decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tasks[] concurrency limiter, model-facing conflict rule, isolate/git-worktree creation, separate child contexts, task results and explicit later review. - Closure path: parent identifies subjobs and overlap risk → chooses disjoint parallel tasks or sequential dependencies plus isolate → distinct child runs on selected paths → independent changes and completion feedback → parent adjusts next delegation/integration. - Boundary reachability: agentTool is a registered executable tool, not a README-only promise; it binds selected isolation to createWorktree and passes the resulting worktreePath to runAgent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent decides actual partition/scheduling from the task context, while the platform creates and enforces the filesystem isolation. No claim of automatic static conflict detection on every invocation. - Evidence: model-facing parallel and same-file exclusion contract, active isolation and child creation, parallel task execution, worktree support. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: isolation is optional and task overlap is inferred by a model from descriptions, not guaranteed by a scheduler; unisolated parallel edits can still collide. This A is a supported explicit model-owner path, not all-mode automatic coverage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the parent mission&#39;s whole current population of running, completed and failed background/foreground child jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child drift, stalled or unnecessary background work, resource/token consumption, changed task constraints and incomplete results in flight. - Whole-system current view: TaskManager maintains the registry; model-facing TaskList can show all running/finished jobs, TaskGet/TaskOutput exposes task progress/details, and completion/message notifications return to parent turn context. - Current-control decision scope: parent model can decide to inspect progress, send mid-course instructions, resume a finished subagent, stop a divergent one or launch replacement work; task state is revised in the current runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task population interventions on live child evidence, rather than only receiving a fixed aggregate report. - Decision owner: model-driven parent receiving these tools; TaskManager stores and executes decisions but cannot autonomously decide priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task registry, AbortController, TaskSend pending-message queue, notification injection, TaskStop, TaskList and on-demand TaskOutput. - Closure path: child task progress/message/error → main context/TaskList → parent model chooses stop/steer/resume/reassign → targeted child receives message or abort → updated state/result returns to main model. - Boundary reachability: agent tool registers async/foreground tasks in shared TaskManager and main runTurn drains notifications; TaskStop and TaskSend use the same manager. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model, not a timeout or UI operator alone, chooses whether specific live jobs should continue, be redirected or stopped. This is parent-mission S3 only, not multi-user organizational governance. - Evidence: task registry, messaging, current snapshot, model-visible TaskList, real TaskStop abort, mid-course TaskSend/resume, main notification return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: current whole is the parent session&#39;s task fleet. Tool descriptions can encourage but do not guarantee a particular model will use supervision at every point.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge a producer&#39;s completion account using a distinct adversarial, read-only project verifier that runs source-specific tests and seeks counterexamples. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementing agent can claim success from its own mocks/happy-path tests or summary while real app/API/CLI/public contract still fails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate verify subagent chooses adversarial cases, directly runs tests/server/browser/CLI checks and returns pass/fail/partial findings for producer rework. - Decision owner: model-backed built-in verify agent with its own role/context/tool restrictions; parent chooses dispatch/repairs. A status parser or fixed VerifyDone command is not the auditing owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verify agent definition, disallowed edit/write tools, read/execute investigation, risk-specific probes, task return/notification, model-facing nontrivial-change verification guidance. - Closure path: producer changes and claim → parent dispatches verify Task → verifier independently reads project/spec and exercises tests/behavior → findings and commands → parent fixes or rechecks → no unverified completion claim. - Boundary reachability: verify is bundled into the active agent definition list and callable by agent agenttype=verify; the bundled verification skill and tool-facing instructions request it for nontrivial changes. It is a supported optional verification mode, not mandatory on every trivial edit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: verifier chooses tests/edge cases based on direct evidence, not merely accepting the producer&#39;s narrative. Ordinary in-band VerifyDone and same-prefix reflection are excluded. - Claim being audited: reported code change meets its actual intended behavior and does not create material regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: parent/producer tool summary, test claims, todo or VerifyDone result. - Complementary access path: fresh read-only verifier context with source/spec/README, actual test commands, server/browser/API probes and observed stdout/behavior. - Independence boundary: distinct model-backed subagent and tool restriction relative to implementing agent, but potentially same external model provider and parent task; no organizationally external audit independence claimed. - Who acts on findings: parent/coding agent repairs or qualifies results after receiving verifier return; verifier does not write project files itself. - Evidence: built-in adversarial verify definition, verify skill dispatch guidance, agent role dispatch and permission, model-facing subagent evidence skepticism. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: optional dispatch and external provider availability; no actual test run of the independent model verifier was observed in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt later coding sessions&#39; operating guidance to recurring user corrections, preferences and observed failed techniques. - Disturbance / variety regulated: outside user negative feedback about current approach or missing acceptance criteria that would otherwise recur across future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed interactive reflector recognizes a correction, proposes a reusable why/how prevention rule, selects memory type/confidence/merge target and causes persistent upsert; startup validation governs whether future sessions receive it. - Decision owner: model-driven unified reflector as a distinct model invocation in the first-party interactive runtime, with deterministic salience/trust/secret filters and memory store as supporting constructors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: correction-specific prompt, feedback why/how criterion, scope routing, mergeInto/upsert persistence, code-based memory validation and stable session prompt injection. - Closure path: user corrects a mistaken approach/DoD → reflector extracts prospective rule with why/how → durable user/project memory upsert or revision → next interactive session filters against live code → accepted guidance injected into model context → subsequent engineering behavior can change. - Boundary reachability: src/index.ts calls maybeReflect during normal interactive turns (unless --no-memory/one-shot), runs unifiedReflect, persists accepted memory and loads/validates/uses memory at next startup. Challenger drift review is a separately opt-in feature and not required for S4 credit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model decides which user reaction is a durable future rule and its specific meaning; validation/GC/filing alone do not create that semantic adaptation. - External distinction: human user correction or a demand for stronger acceptance checks is an external expectation signal, not merely model self-talk or log summarization. - Future / prospective distinction: the extracted why/how rule is intended to guide later unrelated sessions and prevent recurrence, rather than fix only the present code line. - Adaptation option generated: reusable feedback/procedural memory advising changed future checks or mode of work, with confidence and source. - Path back into current capability / S3: validated memory becomes a fixed next-session model prefix and memoryread retrieval source, changing the operating discretion of the next coding/S3 parent; no separate organization-wide S5 right is implied. - Evidence: model-authored user correction and future rule prompt, actual interactive reflection persistence and correction, future-session code validation and prompt injection, memory store. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: contingent on normal interactive memory being enabled and the model extracting a substantive rule; one-shot/--no-memory/benchmark paths skip the loop. No measured longitudinal adaptation was tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous legitimacy/identity policy forum governs the system&#39;s ultimate purpose. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sensitive paths, dangerous commands, project permission modes and skill instruction conflicts are constrained by a predetermined rule hierarchy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the user or managed enterprise setting chooses access/identity constraints; the model can propose tool calls but cannot constitutionally amend ultimate policy. - Decision owner: configured owner/enterprise policy and fixed permission interpreter, not a model-led S5 constitutional decision process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: allow/ask/deny, managed policy precedence, sandbox/secret scanning, constitution-style prompt priorities, permission modes and human confirmations. - Closure path: selected user/managed rules → tool request → permission gate/ask/deny → operational outcome; no autonomous ultimate-purpose deliberation → policy amendment → returned binding authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a priority ordering in the system prompt and layered permission enforcement do not constitute a function that legitimately revises organizational identity. - Evidence: permission hierarchy, permissions engine, settings and managed policy. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within inspected boundary. - Caveats: external owner institutions, DeepSeek provider policy and the governance of user repositories are excluded.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a33beb528485d159214e38ee47a0669b1a87643a/assessments/da/dao-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a33beb528485d159214e38ee47a0669b1a87643a\">a33beb5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T20:33:02Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/octos-org/octos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Octos</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-28\">2026-09-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/octos-org/octos/tree/9f6311afd4e00324cdb89d957461cf861f4f2630\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9f6311afd4e00324cdb89d957461cf861f4f2630\">9f6311a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user objectives into task/repository/application outcomes through repeated model decisions, first-party tool execution and returned observations; child agents and peers can independently execute bounded operational work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user goals, changing workspace state, tool/MCP/plugin results and failures, model-provider responses, delegated subtasks and multi-turn task state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive response/tool actions and choose subsequent actions after observing tool results within the configured policy boundary. - Decision owner: the configured model-driven Octos Agent or autonomous child/peer Agent for its local operational task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: octos-agent loop, first-party tool registry, session/context state, sandbox and permission policy, model adapters, persistence, budgets, cancellation and recovery. - Closure path: objective enters a Session → model receives context/tools → model chooses response/tool calls → first-party runtime executes allowed calls → results are returned into model-visible history → model chooses the next action or completion → durable Session/task state receives the outcome. - Boundary reachability: the model/tool loop is the standard execution kernel used by the shipped CLI/gateway/serve runtime and by first-party child/peer execution paths; it does not depend on an external orchestrator to select each substantive next action. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model decision maker removes the open-ended substantive action selection; deterministic runtime policy can constrain or terminate work but does not reproduce those decisions. - Evidence: README.md; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; book/src/multi-agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent concurrent autonomous peer repository work from destructively interfering through one shared checkout by assigning a peer to its own fenced Git worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more sovereign peer S1s may perform repository mutations in parallel; if they share one checkout, one peer&#39;s writes/index/branch state can contaminate another peer&#39;s observations and work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: when handing off autonomous work, decide whether the peer receives a dedicated worktree/branch so later repository actions are isolated from sibling/current workspace mutation. - Decision owner: the originator Octos Agent invoking the model-visible peerhandoff tool with the worktree option. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-peer durable directories, originator identity, optional Git worktree on peer/&lt;slug&gt;, separate peer Session/workspace and lifecycle. - Closure path: master Agent identifies parallel peer work → invokes peerhandoff with worktree fencing → first-party runtime stages a sovereign peer in the dedicated checkout → that peer&#39;s later repository observations/actions occur in its fenced workspace → sibling/current checkout mutations do not become its live working state. - Boundary reachability: peerhandoff is a first-party agent tool in gateway/serve mode, and the documented worktree argument is part of the supported peer lifecycle at the pinned revision. - Distinct S1 units: sovereign peer Sessions each run their own session/open + turn/start lifecycle and perform independent operational tasks with their own brief/workspace/state. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent repository mutation through a common checkout would couple peer filesystem/Git state and can invalidate or overwrite another peer&#39;s work or observations. - Attenuating coordination relation: originator-selected per-peer Git worktree/branch fencing separates the mutation domains of the autonomous peer S1s. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after staging, each fenced peer&#39;s subsequent file/shell/repository observations and actions resolve against its dedicated worktree, changing what that S1 sees and can interfere with. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited mechanism is not peergather, message delivery, fleet size, scheduling or delegation topology; it attenuates the concrete cross-S1 disturbance of concurrent repository-state interference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime implements worktree isolation, but the originator model has the supported decision right to request the fenced mode for the peer it creates; the same substantive coordination choice is therefore not fixed solely by a constructor policy. - Evidence: book/src/multi-agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: plain peer creation without worktree and generic swarm parallelism are not independently credited as S2. The positive witness is specifically the model-selected fenced-worktree mode for plural repository-operating peers.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain current control over the active multi-agent/task organization by inspecting the whole current peer/task set and changing live commitments or interventions through steer/close/cancel/restart controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: peers/children can be running, done, failed, stalled or producing divergent results; ongoing commitments may require new instructions, retirement, cancellation or targeted restart as current evidence changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which current workers continue, receive new instructions, are retired/cancelled or are restarted, based on whole-set current status/output. - Decision owner: base A mode — the master Octos Agent through model-visible peer supervision tools; parent P mode — the legitimate human/application/higher-recursion controller through first-party OUP supervision and intervention methods. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerlist, peergather, peersendinput, peerclose; backend agent/task lifecycle projections; agent/list, agent/status/read, agent/output/read, task inspection, turn/steer, turn/interrupt, task/cancel and task/restartfromnode where supported. - Closure path: current workers/tasks execute → first-party runtime retains statuses/outputs/artifacts → decisive owner inspects the current set → owner selects an intervention or continuation decision → runtime applies the steer/close/cancel/restart action → subsequent current execution reflects that returned decision. - Boundary reachability: autonomous peer supervision tools are available to the originator Agent in shipped gateway/serve mode; OUP exposes the corresponding runtime-owned state/control surfaces to a supported parent controller without requiring ownership from octoscode itself. - Whole-system current view: in the autonomous peer mode, peerlist enumerates every staged peer and peergather reads named or all peer results; in the parent mode, OUP projects current agent/task/session state and retained outputs/artifacts from the same runtime. - Current-control decision scope: alter current worker commitments and priorities by injecting follow-up work, retiring a peer, interrupting a turn, cancelling a task or restarting a terminal task/node rather than merely enforcing a fixed concurrency or token limit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the base mode, the master model chooses when and how to use the peer controls after reading current state. The parent mode remains distinct because OUP also permits an authorized external parent to inspect and intervene directly; deterministic runtime mechanics only execute the chosen action. - Evidence: book/src/multi-agent.md; README.md; docs/OCTOSUIPROTOCOLCHANGEREQUESTUPCR2026019AGENTSUPERVISION.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review current repository/work-product claims through backend-owned specialist agents and return a joined review into the controlling Session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an ordinary implementation agent may omit defects, policy violations or risky changes from its own report; relying only on the same execution/reporting path would leave those claims unchecked. - Decisive decision or feedback right: specialist review agents autonomously inspect the selected review target and produce findings; the master review join autonomously synthesizes those findings into the Session&#39;s final review result. - Decision owner: first-party model-driven review specialists plus the model-driven master review join. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed review/start, server-resolved specialist list, separate child runtimes, fork-scoped/sanitized child context, independent lifecycle/artifact projection and final joined review persistence. - Closure path: review target is selected → server launches backend-owned specialists separate from ordinary implementation reporting → specialists inspect target and return findings/artifacts → master review join synthesizes them → final review is persisted into the Session → subsequent master/parent control can act on the findings. - Boundary reachability: review/start is an accepted and implemented first-party OUP/runtime feature at the pinned revision, and the same backend can schedule supervised specialists from an ordinary review request. - Claim being audited: correctness/safety/quality of a current repository change or other supported review target, beyond the implementer&#39;s own completion report. - Ordinary reporting path: normal Agent/child output, tool events, task summaries and artifacts produced while doing the work. - Complementary access path: separately launched review specialists inspect the review target in their own supervised child contexts and return review-specific findings/artifacts through the backend review workflow. - Independence boundary: review children are separate runtimes with fork-scoped/sanitized context and server-resolved policies rather than merely re-labeling the implementer&#39;s self-report; the final join consumes their separate outputs. - Who acts on findings: the master review join returns the synthesized result to the controlling Session, after which the autonomous master or legitimate parent can change subsequent work using the established current-control surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic task supervision and artifact retention support the audit, but the substantive review judgments and final synthesis are model-driven specialist/master decisions. - Evidence: docs/OCTOSUIPROTOCOLCHANGEREQUESTUPCR2026019AGENTSUPERVISION.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt reusable skill instructions from observed plugin-tool failures so future agent/tool interactions can avoid a recurring failure mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failures and interface mismatches encountered while operating first-party plugin tools can reveal that current skill guidance is insufficient for future successful use. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether a failure-derived proposed instruction should be applied to the target skill&#39;s durable SKILL.md guidance or discarded. - Decision owner: the autonomous Octos Agent invoking the model-visible skillevolve tool; an LLM-backed hook generates proposals, while the agent owns the apply/discard decision in the credited mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled aftertoolcall hook, reverse mapping from failed plugin tool to skill, LLM suggestion generation, per-skill evolutions.json, skillevolve list/apply/discard/consolidate actions and durable ## Learned Notes writes to SKILL.md. - Closure path: plugin-tool operation fails → bundled hook captures tool/error evidence → LLM generates an instruction intended to prevent recurrence → proposal is stored → Agent inspects pending evolution patches → Agent chooses apply → first-party tool rewrites target SKILL.md with learned guidance → later skill loading/model guidance changes future operational capability. - Boundary reachability: skill-evolve is one of the bundled app-skills auto-installed by the shipped gateway runtime, and its manifest exposes skillevolve as a standard model-visible plugin tool rather than a repository-development-only script. - External distinction: the adaptation begins from an observed plugin-tool interaction failure at the system/environment interface, not from a static roadmap or maintainer backlog. - Future / prospective distinction: the generated suggestion is explicitly framed as an instruction that would prevent recurrence; it is queued as a durable change proposal rather than used only to retry the current call. - Adaptation option generated: an LLM-generated one-line correction to the owning skill&#39;s model-facing instructions, with optional consolidation of accumulated learned notes. - Path back into current capability / S3: applying the proposal writes durable skill guidance used by the harness&#39; skill-loading/model-prompt path, changing how subsequent Agent operation approaches that tool; S3/master operation can then use the adapted skill under the new instruction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: proposal generation is automated but explicitly does not apply patches by itself. The positive A claim rests on the separate first-party model-visible tool through which the Agent itself can inspect the queue and choose apply/discard; without that decision path the hook alone would not justify autonomous S4. - Evidence: crates/app-skills/skill-evolve/SKILL.md; crates/app-skills/skill-evolve/manifest.json; crates/app-skills/skill-evolve/src/main.rs; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: memory refresh, monitors, loops and adaptive provider routing are not independently credited as S4 here. The positive witness is the failure-derived prospective skill-change loop with an agent-owned apply/discard right. Failures that carry no environment-relevant lesson would not themselves establish S4; the credited path is the supported mode where the failure yields a reusable future-facing correction.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and amend the runtime Agent&#39;s authoritative identity/personality/value layer that governs subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the current identity, personality or value definition may no longer match the legitimate workspace/operator&#39;s intended organizational identity and policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the authoritative contents of the custom identity/value bootstrap files that are injected into subsequent model-visible system context. - Decision owner: legitimate parent workspace/operator authority editing .octos/IDENTITY.md and/or .octos/SOUL.md. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bootstrap-file loader, .octos/IDENTITY.md custom identity definition, .octos/SOUL.md personality/values and hot reload into the system prompt. - Closure path: parent identifies an identity/value policy change → parent edits the authoritative bootstrap file → first-party runtime detects/reloads bootstrap content → subsequent Agent turns receive the new identity/value definition in their system prompt → operation proceeds under the returned parent decision. - Boundary reachability: bootstrap identity files are a documented shipped runtime surface created by octos init; their hot-reload path operates inside the standard runtime and does not require octoscode or repository-maintainer governance to enforce the decision. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what identity, personality and values the runtime Agent is to operate under for the workspace/profile. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent P mode — the legitimate workspace/operator authority that owns the bootstrap identity/value files. No autonomous ultimate-authority mode is established. - Return-to-operation path: edited bootstrap content is hot-reloaded and injected into the model-visible system prompt, so subsequent Agent behavior is governed by the new parent decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic filesystem capability does not make the operating Agent the legitimate ultimate authority over its own identity. The documented governance surface assigns definition to workspace bootstrap files edited by the parent/operator; therefore only P is credited. - Evidence: book/src/memory-skills.md; crates/octos-cli/src/configwatcher.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic systemprompt, tool policy, permission profiles, approvals and arbitrary writable files are not independently credited as S5. The positive witness is the explicit identity/value bootstrap surface plus its first-party return-to-operation path.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/21ab146bab92587a437df6c6902e3f61caecbc6f/assessments/octos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 21ab146bab92587a437df6c6902e3f61caecbc6f\">21ab146 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:22:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openJiuwen-ai/jiuwenswarm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">JiuwenSwarm</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openJiuwen-ai/jiuwenswarm/tree/02b37f47fbefe80a58e4ed88606120818ca44ac3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02b37f47fbefe80a58e4ed88606120818ca44ac3\">02b37f4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: deliver delegated research/coding/content artifacts in the task environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source/material, tool failures and task-specific alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: members select methods/tools and revise work from observations. - Decision owner: model-driven first-party-composed agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pinned DeepAgent/Runner, session/workspace state, tools, permission rails and transport. - Closure path: task/request → member model actions → environment observations → revised actions and delivered artifacts. - Boundary reachability: TeamManager.getswarmenrichedteamspec calls enrichteamspecforswarm and produces runnable specs for supported Cluster mode; Agent mode is also documented. Dependency membership is established by pyproject.toml. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actors leaves transport/enforcement without equivalent task discretion. - Evidence: jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/team/teammanager.py; jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/team/configloader.py; docs/en/AgentTeam.md; openjiuwen/agentteams/prompts/en/teammatepolicy.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-artifact write interference and resolve coordination deadlock among members. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple members writing the same shared deliverable, with lock conflicts and possibly incompatible local choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: members acquire/release scopes and negotiate with peers; unresolved directional conflicts go to the Leader. - Decision owner: member agents, with Leader arbitration for deadlock. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workspacemeta lock/owner metadata, separate workspaces, message delivery and task state. - Closure path: before shared write → member requests exclusive lock → conflict names holder → peer negotiation/wait/revised action → unlock and resumed work. - Boundary reachability: the target builds TeamAgentSpec and workspace tooling through pinned Core; the shipped teammate prompt explicitly requires workspacemeta locks for shared deliverables. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: locks themselves are deterministic; members choose when/what to lock and how to respond to the other holder. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently executing teammates delivering different task outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writes to the same shared artifact, explicitly identified in the teammate collaboration policy and tool&#39;s Locked by response. - Attenuating coordination relation: cooperative exclusive-lock discipline plus lateral messages and deadlock escalation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: lock failure reveals the holder so the agent changes write timing/scope or negotiates; release permits subsequent writes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation targets a concrete shared-write collision rather than treating the mailbox or task DAG itself as coordination. - Evidence: openjiuwen/agentteams/prompts/en/teammatepolicy.md; openjiuwen/agentteams/teamworkspace/tools.py; docs/en/AgentTeam.md; jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/team/teammanager.py. - Caveats: cooperative lock discipline is agent-owned and is not automatic writefile exclusion; no claim that all actual conflicts are prevented. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate present whole-team commitments and intervene in stalled or repeatedly rejected work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: capability mismatch, directional conflict, exhausted review rounds and stalled commitments threatening the team goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reassign, change task constraints/review setup, stop a stalled member or replan current commitments. - Decision owner: Leader model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task/member tools, event-driven mailbox/scheduler, snapshot state, permission narrowing and runtime lifecycle controls. - Closure path: team state/escalation plus assignee account → Leader judgment → updatetask/reassign/reviewer/member change → new operational work. - Boundary reachability: TeamManager constructs the supported team spec; pinned Core supplies Leader policy and scheduled escalation machinery selected by the target&#39;s dispatchmode configuration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Leader selects the intervention; scheduler limits and shutdown execution enforce rather than originate that judgment. - Whole-system current view: Leader sees team roster, task graph/status, member reports, directional conflicts and exhausted/stalled review escalations. - Current-control decision scope: team-wide commitments, task ownership, member capabilities and constraints, not merely the initial task decomposition. - Evidence: openjiuwen/agentteams/prompts/en/leaderpolicy.md; openjiuwen/agentteams/prompts/en/dispatchscheduledleader.md; openjiuwen/agentteams/agent/scheduling/scheduler.py; jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/team/teammanager.py; jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/team/rails/teampermissionpolicyrail.py. - Caveats: generic user pause buttons and fixed token budgets are not credited as parent S3. No distinct parent control mode is claimed. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: adversarial complementary challenge beyond author claims and routine acceptance verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blind spots, faulty assumptions, security risks and blocking failures missed by ordinary production reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: temporary challenger independently inspects the deliverable and casts a blocking pass/fail judgment. - Decision owner: separate challenger model actor; Leader acts on escalated failures. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh temporary harness, raw filesystem tools, reviewer-scoped verifytask identity, deterministic tally and rework routing. - Closure path: author submits → separate challenger examines raw deliverable → fail returns feedback and inprogress rework; exhausted rounds escalate to Leader for changed control. - Boundary reachability: target configloader passes dispatchmode and enabletaskverification into TeamAgentSpec. In supported scheduled mode with verification enabled the Leader can select challenger; Core scheduler builds and runs it with the team&#39;s filesystem tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the challenger owns adversarial judgment; vote parsing and state transitions are enforcement. Plain logs, routine verifier checks and the task-level AutoReviewer permission prompt are not the credited audit. - Claim being audited: the submitted task deliverable is acceptable and free of blocking unconsidered defects. - Ordinary reporting path: author completion report and routine verifier/acceptance checks. - Complementary access path: a separate adversarial reviewer reads the deliverable with raw filesystem tools and tests threat scenarios beyond the acceptance rubric. - Independence boundary: temporary reviewer has its own system prompt/context and reviewer-scoped task manager; the author cannot vote as this reviewer. Shared model infrastructure does not make it the author&#39;s self-check. - Who acts on findings: author receives rework feedback; Leader receives stalled/exhausted review plus assignee summary. - Evidence: jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/team/configloader.py; openjiuwen/agentteams/agent/scheduling/scheduler.py; openjiuwen/agentteams/prompts/en/reviewerchallenger.md; openjiuwen/agentteams/prompts/en/dispatchscheduledleader.md. - Caveats: verification is disabled by default in the target loader; A describes an explicitly supported enabled mode, not every default run. Routine verifier-only configurations do not establish this S3 witness. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt reusable capability to externally supplied corrections and changing recurring task requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a reusable mismatch between a skill and user/environment requirements, beyond a transient task failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which reusable update to retain and apply to future skill use. - Decision owner: base mode: agent/evolution actor with enabled autosave; parent mode: local operator approves specific proposed experience records. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trajectory evidence, evolution review/proposal schema, global skill experience store, approval interruption and future skill loading. - Closure path: task/user correction → assess reusability → structured skill improvement → auto-save or operator approval → persisted experience loaded on later skill calls. - Boundary reachability: Swarm evolution providers explicitly construct/register TeamSkillEvolutionRail and EvolutionInterruptRail. Enabled feature and autosave settings select the complete alternative modes; no custom implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-generated option selection plus automatic retention closes the base mode; when autosave=false the operator owns the decisive retention judgment, hence separately evidenced parent mode. - External distinction: user correction or recurring usage requirement that reveals a reusable gap in a skill, explicitly distinguished from personal preference and one-off environment errors. - Future / prospective distinction: the actor judges applicability to later tasks of the same kind and develops reusable prechecks/fallback/verification guidance. - Adaptation option generated: structured skill experience proposal naming target/section/reason/content, or a reusable team skill improvement. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved/saved experience is kept in the shared active skill library and loaded on subsequent skill calls, changing the response repertoire available to the team. - Evidence: docs/en/SkillSelfEvolution.md; jiuwenswarm/agents/swarm/providers/evolutionrails.py; openjiuwen/harness/rails/evolution/skillevolutionrail.py; openjiuwen/harness/rails/evolution/evolutioninterruptrail.py. - Caveats: skillevolution defaults false; only enabled supported modes are credited. Generic memory or internal retry learning alone is not S4. This is structural capability adaptation, not measured improvement. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persona, tool permissions and member authority limits are configured constraints rather than demonstrated ultimate-policy disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: persona editing, tool approval and skill adaptation are not by themselves identity-level adjudication. - Decision owner: operators/developers configure boundaries; Leader owns bounded current operations, not demonstrated ultimate authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: IDENTITY/SOUL templates, AvatarPromptRail, permission narrowing and approval rails. - Closure path: ordinary configuration/approvals return to execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy matter-to-authority-to-return loop is reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a prompt called identity and a human approval mechanism do not establish S5. - Evidence: jiuwenswarm/resources/agent/workspace/IDENTITYEN.md; jiuwenswarm/resources/agent/workspace/SOULEN.md; jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/common/rails/avatarrail.py; jiuwenswarm/agents/harness/team/rails/teampermissionpolicyrail.py; openjiuwen/agentteams/prompts/en/leaderpolicy.md. - Caveats: bounded absence at the operated-harness recursion; contributor governance and a user&#39;s broader institutional authority are outside. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f846936591735470b905ac70e6c0600d20704b42/assessments/ji/jiuwenswarm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f846936591735470b905ac70e6c0600d20704b42\">f846936 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T15:25:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/first-fluke/oh-my-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/first-fluke/oh-my-agent/tree/428e80e93fa9e4e6e9776e755ee57af4ba5cd929\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 428e80e93fa9e4e6e9776e755ee57af4ba5cd929\">428e80e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform specialist software-engineering transformations through autonomous implementation/review agent runs inside OMA-defined task, workspace and acceptance contracts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: codebase-specific implementation choices, local failures, tool/runtime variation, specialist-domain uncertainty and task-specific repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each spawned implementation agent chooses its local sequence of coding/tool actions and produces a run-scoped result against its assigned acceptance contract. - Decision owner: the executing specialist agent actor launched through an OMA native or cross-vendor dispatch path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured plan tasks, agent definitions, oma agent spawn, native dispatch adapters, isolated workspaces, result contract, progress/result artifacts and verification receipts. - Closure path: authorized task → OMA creates/loads executable plan → specialist agent is dispatched with task/contracts/context → agent autonomously performs local implementation actions → changed files/result evidence return to OMA → result enters subsequent coordination/control/review. - Boundary reachability: automated orchestrate and ultrawork directly spawn supported implementation agents; no downstream adopter must invent the operational work-unit relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: OMA supplies organizational constraints and transport, while the specialist agent makes substantive local implementation decisions rather than replaying a fixed deterministic script. - Evidence: .agents/workflows/orchestrate.md, .agents/workflows/ultrawork.md, shared result-contract/dispatch surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: model/vendor internals remain external. Credit is limited to the standard OMA-created agent work unit and its first-party task/result boundary, not undocumented capabilities of Claude/Codex/etc.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete interference among specialist S1s, especially API/data-contract divergence and workspace ownership conflict, while preserving parallel local work where safe. - Disturbance / variety regulated: backend/frontend/mobile agents can diverge on shared API/data contracts or modify overlapping workspace areas; dependency timing can expose downstream agents to unstable upstream contracts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect whether contracts/workspace ownership are conflicting, pause or defer affected downstream work, reconcile the shared contract, split ownership boundaries, then resume/re-dispatch affected S1s. - Decision owner: the root coordinating agent executing the required OMA coordination/orchestrate workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PM decomposition, priority tiers, shared API/data contracts, task board, separate workspaces/worktrees, progress/result state and coordination rules. - Closure path: multiple specialist S1s operate against shared contract/workspace boundaries → coordinator reads progress and contract state → concrete divergence/conflict is detected → coordinator pauses/repartitions/reconciles → revised contract/ownership/instructions return to affected agents before subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: automated orchestrate is required to read the first-party coordination skill before fan-out, carries contracts to each agent, and monitors alignment; the qualifying path is therefore reachable without the user manually coordinating every worker. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worktree isolation and tier scheduling are enforcement mechanisms, but the model coordinator owns the contextual judgment that a concrete contract/ownership disturbance exists and selects/revises the response. - Evidence: .agents/skills/oma-coordination/SKILL.md, .agents/workflows/orchestrate.md, .agents/workflows/ultrawork.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary parallel dispatch is not the witness; S2 rests on the explicit conflict modes plus corrective feedback to the affected S1s. - Distinct S1 units: backend, frontend, mobile and other specialist implementation agents dispatched as separate task/workspace units under one OMA session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared API/data contracts can diverge across specialists and overlapping workspace ownership can cause conflicting mutations or downstream work against an unstable upstream contract. - Attenuating coordination relation: the root coordinator monitors contract alignment, pauses dependent downstream work until reconciliation, and splits/reassigns ownership boundaries when workspaces conflict. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: reconciled contracts, revised ownership and re-dispatch instructions are supplied back to affected specialist agents before they resume or retry work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is tied to named cross-S1 interference modes and changes the affected units specifically to damp those disturbances; task routing and parallel spawning alone are excluded.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain cohesion over the current multi-agent work portfolio by regulating active commitments, priority/dependency progression, failed/stalled work, retry/re-dispatch decisions and bounded recovery resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent failure, crash, silent no-artifact exit, stalled progress, incomplete acceptance evidence, changed specialist need, retry exhaustion and session cost pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether current work counts as completed, must be preserved/retried, should be reclassified to another specialist, resumed, re-dispatched or stopped as partial/failed within current budget and authorization. - Decision owner: the root OMA coordinating agent in automated orchestrate/ultrawork paths. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: whole-session task board, plan priority/dependency state, process/run status, progress/result artifacts, result contract, retry/recovery budget, quota cap and phase gates. - Closure path: active tasks/runs populate the task board → coordinator polls process health and result evidence across the session → distinguishes completion/failure/no-artifact/stall and current constraints → selects recovery/reclassification/re-dispatch/stop → OMA executes the changed commitment decision and updates shared state → later scheduling/reporting uses the new current portfolio. - Boundary reachability: /orchestrate is explicitly an automated execution mode; first-party state and dispatch commands expose the whole-set view and intervention rights to its coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates/caps constrain options, but the coordinating agent interprets heterogeneous current evidence and owns discretionary portfolio interventions such as reclassification and re-dispatch. - Evidence: .agents/workflows/orchestrate.md, .agents/workflows/ultrawork.md, .agents/skills/oma-coordination/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: task decomposition alone is not credited; the positive mapping depends on the subsequent whole-session monitoring and intervention loop. - Whole-system current view: task-board/session state plus run/progress/result evidence across all planned agents. - Current-control decision scope: priority/dependency progression, acceptance of completed commitments, reclassification, retry/re-dispatch, preservation of partial work and bounded stop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation/current-control claims using fresh-context reviewers and mechanically grounded artifact evidence, then return findings into corrective operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementer self-report, shared-context anchoring, rationalized shortcuts, stale PASS claims, regressions, missing execution phases and acceptance-criteria violations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently classify evidence as PASS/FAIL/REGRESSED/BLOCKED or identify review findings that invalidate claimed completion. - Decision owner: separately spawned fresh-context QA/reviewer/JUDGE agent(s), not the implementation agent or ordinary coordinator context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CCR isolation contract, per-review fresh subagents, Ralph JUDGE protocol, evidence-only briefs, artifact paths/diffs/tests, anti-circumvention verifier, regression state transitions and run-scoped result artifacts. - Closure path: S1/S3 produces implementation and ordinary completion claims → independent reviewer/judge receives complementary raw artifacts/evidence without implementer narration → reviewer returns verdict/findings → FAIL/REGRESSED changes criterion/task state and sends work to REPLAN or back to implementation → corrected work is re-verified before completion. - Boundary reachability: fresh-context review is mandatory in ultrawork; Ralph&#39;s spawned judge is the default operating path, with inline judging explicitly recorded only as an independence downgrade fallback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is produced by an independent reviewer agent. Deterministic state-transition/artifact machinery transports/enforces that judgment but does not substitute for the auditor. - Evidence: .agents/workflows/ultrawork.md, .agents/workflows/ralph.md, .agents/workflows/ralph/resources/judge-protocol.md, .agents/workflows/orchestrate.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: repository benchmark scorers are excluded; the positive witness is the deployed workflow&#39;s structurally independent reviewer/judge path and corrective return. - Claim being audited: implementation agents and the ordinary OMA coordinator claim that planned work/phases satisfy acceptance criteria and are ready to advance or complete. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation agent result/progress artifacts plus the coordinator&#39;s normal task-board, phase and completion state. - Complementary access path: fresh-context reviewer/JUDGE agents independently read raw diff, changed files, plan, checks, acceptance evidence and required phase artifacts while excluding implementer narration and reasoning. - Independence boundary: reviewer agents are separately spawned in fresh isolated contexts; Ralph explicitly forbids the orchestrator from self-judging except a recorded downgraded fallback. - Who acts on findings: the OMA coordinator changes criterion/task state and returns FAIL/REGRESSED findings to REPLAN or the responsible implementation agent for corrective work and re-verification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert evidence from failed/blocked/partial project runs into evaluated skill adaptations that change later OMA behavior on similar work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring mismatch between current skill guidance and project-environment acceptance contracts, observed failed outputs, negative transfer risk and changes in the managed skill base. - Decisive decision or feedback right: derive/attribute a regression case from recorded operational evidence, generate candidate skill adaptations, evaluate alternatives, and install a passing project overlay in enabled apply mode. - Decision owner: first-party optimization agent/path owns candidate adaptation generation; evaluation gates qualify the result and the enabled scheduled evolution controller applies the passing candidate automatically. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tracked run evidence, acceptance contracts, incident capture/promotion, judge rubrics, skill attribution/routing, optimizer, train/validation/final-test/isolation/negative-transfer checks, finite dispatch budget, persistent retry state, scheduler, overlay conflict checks and rollback history. - Closure path: real project run fails/blocks/partially completes against a recorded acceptance contract → scheduled feedback captures and validates the failure as a regression fixture → affected skill is identified → optimizer generates an adaptation candidate → held-in/held-out/final-test and negative-transfer gates evaluate it → default enabled apply mode installs a project skill overlay → subsequent sessions resolve the effective skill body through the overlay and operate with changed capability. - Boundary reachability: oma harness evolution enable --max-dispatches ... registers the built-in scheduler; the documented default schedule is daily and default mode is apply. After this explicit deployment choice, cycles close without per-change human approval. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the adaptation candidate is generated through the first-party optimization path from environment-relevant failure distinctions. Deterministic gates and scheduler constrain and operationalize the choice rather than inventing the semantic adaptation. - Evidence: web/docs/guide/harness-incidents.md, web/docs/guide/harness-evolution.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary memory, lessons and repository self-development are not the witness. S4 is credited only to the opt-in deployed project-evolution mode whose accepted changes feed future project operation. - External distinction: actual project tasks, acceptance contracts, preserved outputs and failure evidence reveal where the current harness/skill fails to cope with its project environment. - Future / prospective distinction: each validated incident becomes a regression case used to form and test a skill revision intended to prevent recurrence on later tasks. - Adaptation option generated: candidate project skill overlay/diff with recorded parent/candidate hashes and evaluation evidence. - Path back into current capability / S3: a passing overlay becomes the effective project skill body; later OMA sessions use it in operational/coordination/control workflows, while conflicts/rollback preserve control over present capability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the reviewed OMA recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user authorization, scope limits, build permission, cost caps, constitutions, workflow invariants and approval/clarification paths constrain work but remain ordinary operational/governance boundaries rather than identity-level adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue at the OMA organization level. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution policy, user authorization, clarification protocol, phase gates, cost/retry caps, static constitutions/configuration and explicit approval requirements for out-of-scope/irreversible work. - Closure path: not applicable; inspected human and policy paths approve or bound ordinary task execution but do not demonstrate an identity/purpose conflict reaching legitimate ultimate authority and returning as organization-level policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: OMA&#39;s coordinator and workers operate under supplied purpose and authorization. They do not own ultimate identity authority, and ordinary human final say over task scope is explicitly insufficient for S5 under the Profile. - Evidence: .agents/skills/shared/core/execution-policy.md, orchestrate/ultrawork/ralph workflow gates, configuration/constitution surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6849b88c98788b0b482e9d43ac92c23650503d6b/assessments/oh-my-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6849b88c98788b0b482e9d43ac92c23650503d6b\">6849b88 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T19:28:16Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yeachan-Heo/oh-my-claudecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-claudecode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yeachan-Heo/oh-my-claudecode/tree/5281b19e0d64f8e6dc6767f2130299a88af2dc71\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5281b19e0d64f8e6dc6767f2130299a88af2dc71\">5281b19 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work on the target repository through autonomous file/module-scoped workers that inspect, edit, test and debug assigned outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous implementation/debugging/documentation/test tasks, source state, test/build failures, dependency constraints and repository feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: within assigned scope, choose concrete inspection, edit, command, test and debugging actions needed to produce the requested software outcome. - Decision owner: model-backed OMC worker agents such as executor, debugger, designer, writer and test-engineer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code Agent/Task execution; OMC worker prompts; task ownership; shell/file tools; task state; optional worktrees; provider routing. - Closure path: lead assigns bounded outcome → worker inspects repository and chooses actions → repository/test output returns → worker iterates to completion/failure → evidence returns to Team lead for verification/correction. - Boundary reachability: native /team is canonical documented OMC runtime; team-exec explicitly spawns these workers and their results drive the pipeline. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model-backed workers leaves task/state/tool machinery but removes open-ended local engineering decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/SKILL.md; agents/executor.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Claude/model inference is externally provided, but OMC packages the role contracts and runtime path invoking those actors.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrently operating workers through ownership/dependency decisions and conflict responses that return into worker behaviour. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate task claiming, overlapping file edits, dependency races, branch/worktree collisions and merge/rebase conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task/file ownership/dependency ordering and, when collisions occur, decide which worker should own/retry/rebase/defer affected work or which merge strategy to take. - Decision owner: the model-backed Team lead in native Team mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pre-assigned owners; blockedBy; file/module scoping; worktree isolation; conflict detection/mailboxes; task updates; worker messages; deterministic allocation helpers. - Closure path: collision/race becomes visible → lead selects ownership/dependency/reassignment/conflict response → task/inbox/worktree state changes → affected workers follow revised assignment or resolve/defer collision → later evidence returns to lead. - Boundary reachability: native Team instructs the lead to pre-assign owners, monitor concurrent workers, reassign work and handle conflict/failure cases as part of the bundled workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic worktree/mailbox/allocation mechanisms transport/enforce coordination, but native Team leaves substantive cross-worker ownership/retry/conflict choices to the lead model. - Evidence: skills/team/SKILL.md; src/team/conflict-mailbox.ts; src/team/allocation-policy.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: delegation/least-load routing alone is not the witness; the mapping relies on explicit race/file/merge disturbances plus lead-owned responses. - Distinct S1 units: multiple simultaneously spawned Team workers, each owning a file/module-scoped software outcome and model/tool loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Team explicitly records non-atomic claim races and bounded file ownership; worktree/merge support exposes concrete concurrent file and branch conflicts. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead pre-assignment, dependency ordering, bounded file scope, reassignment and conflict-strategy decisions, with worktree isolation/conflict checks as enforcement. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: updated owner/dependency records and lead messages determine what each worker works on next; conflict instructions pause/redirect affected workers until resolved. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation is tied to explicit cross-worker race/edit/merge collision modes and changes later worker behaviour to prevent or resolve them.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current whole-team cohesion by observing the live worker/task set and intervening in current commitments/capacity when workers fail, stall, die or leave dependencies blocked. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uneven/idle load, stuck tasks, worker death, repeated failures, blocked dependencies, failed tasks and incomplete commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: from the whole-team view, decide retry versus reassign versus skip, change ownership/dependencies, stop assigning to a repeatedly failing worker, spawn replacement or proceed to shutdown/verification. - Decision owner: the model-backed Team lead. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: getTeamStatus; task-list polling; messages/outbox; heartbeat/liveness state; watchdog timing; failure counters; task updates; spawn/shutdown; deterministic router/allocation policy. - Closure path: whole-set exception becomes visible → lead chooses present-time intervention → owner/dependency/worker set or commitment changes → workers execute revised commitments → later status/completion/failure evidence returns to lead. - Boundary reachability: monitoring, reassignment and error handling are explicit bundled native-Team duties while tasks are live. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: watchdog/runtime state detect or enforce exceptions but do not make materially the same retry/reassign/skip/dependency choice if the lead model is removed. - Evidence: skills/team/SKILL.md; src/team/task-router.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial decomposition/worker selection alone is not credited; witness is live whole-team exception regulation after work is underway. - Whole-system current view: Team monitoring combines all workers&#39; registration/liveness, current task, team-wide pending/in-progress/completed counts and recent messages. - Current-control decision scope: lead can alter live ownership/dependencies, retry/skip work, replace dead workers, withhold assignments from repeatedly failing workers and terminate/progress current Team phase.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge worker completion/quality claims through a separate read-only verification/review pass that gathers fresh repository evidence and returns findings into corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: executor self-approval, stale/unsupported completion claims, acceptance-criteria gaps, regressions and risk-specific security/quality defects not reliably exposed by ordinary worker status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently judge PASS/FAIL/INCOMPLETE or issue risk/quality findings from fresh tests, diagnostics, builds, source inspection and conditional specialist audits. - Decision owner: model-backed verifier and, when selected by risk/scale, separate security-reviewer / code-reviewer agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewer contracts; prohibited Write/Edit; fresh test/build/LSP commands; OWASP/secrets/dependency audits; stage routing; verify/fix transition and bounded retry loop. - Closure path: workers report execution results → separate verifier/reviewer independently inspects actual repository/evidence → PASS permits progression, failed findings generate fix tasks → Team enters team-fix → separate executors correct work → another verification pass. - Boundary reachability: team-verify is canonical Team stage; verifier is always routed after execution and risk-sensitive/large changes explicitly add specialist reviewers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime transitions enforce verdict consequences, but substantive acceptance/risk judgment is produced by separate model-backed audit actors that cannot author the change they audit. - Evidence: agents/verifier.md; agents/security-reviewer.md; agents/code-reviewer.md; skills/team/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary executor tests are not the witness; mapping relies on separated no-self-approval review plus conditional risk/scale reviewers with materially different access. - Claim being audited: completed worker changes actually satisfy original acceptance criteria without fresh test/build/type failures, regressions or risk-specific defects. - Ordinary reporting path: worker completion/failure and implementation evidence return through Team task/message state to lead. - Complementary access path: after authoring, read-only verifier independently runs fresh tests/build/LSP and reads repository evidence rather than trusting worker claims; security-sensitive or large/architectural changes additionally receive specialized audit. - Independence boundary: reviewer roles are separate from authoring, verifier explicitly forbids self-approval and credited reviewer roles disallow Write/Edit. - Who acts on findings: Team lead/pipeline consumes verdicts; failed verification creates fix work for separate executor/debugger actors before re-verification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model external/future-relevant improvement opportunities, develop competing adaptation hypotheses and return experimentally validated improvements into capability used by later self-improve rounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing techniques/dependencies, unknown optimization opportunities, failed prior approaches, plateaus and uncertainty over which future change will improve target capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: develop testable improvement hypotheses from repository/history/external evidence, turn them into candidate changes and carry accepted evidence-backed options into the improvement branch. - Decision owner: distributed model-backed self-improve arrangement: researcher/planners own semantic research/hypothesis generation and executors instantiate candidates; deterministic tournament rules rank/confirm those agent-generated options by benchmark. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research briefs/history; external search; planner/architect/critic agents; experiment worktrees; sealed benchmark; tournament ranking/re-benchmark; merge/revert; best-score/plateau/circuit-breaker state. - Closure path: current repository/history plus external research → researcher/planners generate future options → executors implement hypotheses in worktrees → benchmark/tournament validates winner → winner merges into improve/{goalslug} → next iteration branches from updated state and uses accumulated history/results. - Boundary reachability: /self-improve dispatches to bundled skill; after explicit setup/trust configuration the documented loop runs autonomously until predefined stop conditions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: benchmark ranking/merge gates enforce evidence, but without researcher/planner/executor model actors the open-ended external interpretation and hypothesis-generation/adaptation repertoire disappears. - Evidence: skills/self-improve/SKILL.md; skills/self-improve/si-researcher.md; commands/self-improve.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic task planning/model routing/learned-skill storage are not credited; witness is external/prospective research → competing hypotheses → experiment → persistent winner → next-round closure. - External distinction: dedicated researcher inspects dependencies/repository evidence and may search external papers, benchmarks, similar projects and official documentation, producing cited ideas rather than relying only on internal task state. - Future / prospective distinction: strategy changes after failures, approach-family exhaustion or proximity to target; planners generate testable future hypotheses and alternative future states are evaluated before adoption. - Adaptation option generated: approved plans become concrete experiment-branch changes across architecture, training/configuration, data, infrastructure, optimization, testing or other approach families. - Path back into current capability / S3: benchmark-confirmed winner merges into persistent improvement branch; later experiments branch from that improved state while best-score/history feeds later research/planning.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop with legitimate ultimate authority and returned operational closure was established at the selected OMC recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: OMC exposes static policies, permissions, governance fields, approval gates and user-selected self-improve goal/benchmark/harness constraints, but these define/approve ordinary operation rather than resolve identity-level tension between current control and future adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5-specific runtime right was found to reinterpret OMC&#39;s ultimate purpose/ethos or adjudicate an identity/ultimate-policy issue and return that ruling into subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt policy; Team governance configuration; graph/action approvals; permissions; self-improve goal/benchmark/harness setup and trust confirmation; static stop/guardrail conditions. - Closure path: no S5-specific closure established. Setup choices/approvals bound ordinary execution; once self-improve begins, its policy forbids pausing for user confirmation between iterations and stops on predefined conditions rather than escalating identity/policy questions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents optimize/regulate inside already chosen task/governance/goal boundaries; static developer/user constraints and action approvals do not form an ultimate-policy loop. - Evidence: src/team/governance.ts; skills/self-improve/SKILL.md; src/agents/prompt-ssot/sections.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user is ultimate external authority over invocation/configuration, but generic setup/action approval/out-of-band modification is not S5 without a first-party identity/ultimate-policy escalation-and-return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ea59f6b2528e8ad42ef154e8e50f3a1746755169/assessments/oh-my-claudecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ea59f6b2528e8ad42ef154e8e50f3a1746755169\">ea59f6b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T17:29:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/autohandai/code-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Autohand Code CLI</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/autohandai/code-cli/tree/feaa8df7dcc5ee0152bc0d961bc271759fced45a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision feaa8df7dcc5ee0152bc0d961bc271759fced45a\">feaa8df &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing engineering transformations by interpreting the objective, selecting permitted tools/actions, observing results and iterating toward the requested outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code/worktree state, implementation alternatives, provider uncertainty, tool/build/test failures, repository instructions and evidence discovered during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific repository/tool action and revise it from returned evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed Autohand lead or delegated teammate/SubAgent within its bounded task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool manager/action executor, permissions, conversation/session state, provider adapters, skills, hooks, context controls and run budgets. - Closure path: objective/evidence → model selects action → first-party executor runs it → result returns into conversation → same actor selects another action or completes. - Boundary reachability: first-party lead and delegated SubAgent loops are shipped across supported CLI/headless/protocol modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves tools, permissions and persistence but removes open-ended task-specific coding discretion. - Evidence: README.md; src/core/agent.ts; src/core/agents/SubAgent.ts; src/core/toolManager.ts at the frozen revision. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: high-risk actions can be operator-governed in some interactive permission modes, but first-party autonomous operation is also supported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrently operating teammate/peer S1 units through dependency-aware work admission, exclusive task ownership and explicit capacity-one resource coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate ownership of current work, execution before prerequisites complete, and simultaneous use of a shared exclusive resource by multiple active agents. - Distinct S1 units: teammate child processes and delegated SubAgent executions each run independent model/tool loops against the shared organization/workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: active units can otherwise claim the same work, consume one exclusive resource simultaneously, or begin a dependent task before another S1 has produced prerequisite state. - Attenuating coordination relation: TaskManager exposes one owner/status per task, blockedBy dependencies and availability only after dependencies complete; TeamManager assigns available tasks to idle teammates. coordinateresource adds capacity-one request/grant/release/cancel/controller semantics for contested resources. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: task completion/status unblocks later tasks and triggers assignment; blocked work is withheld; resource grant/release changes whether another peer may proceed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive witness is concrete duplicate/dependency/resource contention among live S1s and a first-party relation that changes which S1 may act next. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the autonomous lead decides task decomposition/dependency/assignment interventions through team tools while task/resource protocols return contention and availability feedback. - Decision owner: model-backed team lead for task-specific coordination discretion; first-party managers enforce selected dependency/ownership/resource state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager, TeamManager, peer messaging, coordinateresource, team tools and assignment/recovery machinery. - Closure path: lead/peers establish work/resource relation → coordination state accepts/blocks/assigns/grants → workers receive permitted work/resource access → completion/release/status changes coordination state → subsequent S1 behavior changes. - Boundary reachability: teams, task tools and peer/resource tools are standard first-party runtime surfaces at the frozen ref. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic state does not choose task-specific decomposition, dependencies or interventions if the lead actor is removed. - Evidence: docs/teams.md; src/core/teams/TaskManager.ts; src/core/teams/TeamManager.ts; src/core/peerTools.ts; src/core/toolManager.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the positive state is bounded to explicit task/dependency/resource-contention regulation, not a claim that every filesystem collision is prevented.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current organization-wide team population, work commitments, dependencies, assignments and interventions while teammate S1s continue independently. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing team capacity, idle/working/crashed members, blocked/current/completed work, failed or obsolete commitments, dependency changes and cross-unit current priorities. - Whole-system current view: the lead owns active TeamManager/TaskManager state; teamstatus, tasklist, taskget, taskoutput and team view expose the active team, member status, task list, dependencies, progress and outputs. Teammate updates return into this state. - Current-control decision scope: the lead can create/reuse a team, add teammates, create/update tasks/dependencies, inspect current status/output, stop active tasks, send team messages and stop/shut down the team; these actions change current subordinate commitments and allocation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise which current subordinate commitments exist, their dependencies/ownership and when active work/team must be stopped or redirected. - Decision owner: model-backed Autohand team lead. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamManager, TaskManager, teammate processes, process leases, message routing, run/cancel wiring, status snapshots and assignment/recovery machinery. - Closure path: current team/task/member state → lead model chooses intervention → first-party team tool mutates commitment state → teammate behavior/status changes → updated status/output returns to whole-system view. - Boundary reachability: the complete team lifecycle is first-party documented runtime behavior reachable from the standard CLI lead session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the lead, managers retain persistence/availability calculations but not task-specific whole-team composition, dependency, intervention and commitment decisions. - Evidence: docs/teams.md; src/core/toolManager.ts; src/core/teams/TaskManager.ts; src/core/teams/TeamManager.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic auto-assignment enforces available work after the lead defines task/dependency structure; it does not replace the lead&#39;s S3 decision right.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the ordinary implementation path through a separate read-only reviewer that directly inspects repository/change artifacts and returns evidence-based findings to the lead for corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects, regressions, security issues, missing tests, maintainability failures or unsupported completion claims that can survive producer self-reporting and ordinary checks. - Claim being audited: that the scoped code/change is correct, secure, adequately tested and maintainable enough to proceed. - Ordinary reporting path: lead/implementer/teammate performs implementation through its normal coding loop and returns task status/output/tests. - Complementary access path: the embedded reviewer is an independent read-only reviewer with repository/Git tools and an evidence-led contract; delegation instantiates it as a separate SubAgent with its own conversation/model/tool loop and direct repository access. - Independence boundary: the reviewer cannot edit through its shipped tool set, has separate context and receives review scope rather than merely accepting the producer summary. - Who acts on findings: findings return from reviewer to lead/delegating control; the lead can then assign/perform corrective edits, tests, another review or other current-control action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer model owns audit judgment; lead owns corrective operational response. - Decision owner: autonomous first-party reviewer actor for audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: embedded built-in registry/assets; SubAgent; read-only reviewer tools; delegation/team handoff; result transport. - Closure path: lead delegates review → separate reviewer inspects actual diff/repository evidence → reviewer forms findings → findings return to lead → lead changes subsequent implementation/control. - Boundary reachability: reviewer definition is embedded in shipped built-in assets and the generic first-party SubAgent path executes built-ins directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing reviewer model while retaining tools/transport removes independent audit judgment. - Evidence: src/generated/builtinAssets.ts; src/core/agents/SubAgent.ts; docs/teams.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic codereview tooling is not the witness; the state rests on the separately instantiated embedded reviewer actor and corrective handoff.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: model project/external capability conditions, identify future-relevant capability gaps/options and persist selected/generated skills so subsequent Autohand operation has an adapted capability set. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project languages/frameworks/dependencies/testing/CI and patterns change; installed skills can become stale/redundant; external/community options may fit or fail; missing specialized capability can constrain future execution. - External distinction: ProjectAnalyzer distinguishes the current project&#39;s languages, frameworks, dependencies, patterns, platform, Git/tests/CI and package manager; /learn additionally obtains a community skill registry and compares external capability options with the installed set. - Future / prospective distinction: generated skills are explicitly intended for later agent runs, while /learn update detects project evolution using stored/current project hashes and decides whether previously generated capability has become stale relative to the new project state. - Adaptation option generated: model-backed LearnAdvisor audits installed skills, ranks community matches, performs capability-gap analysis and can generate a tailored new skill; --auto-skill likewise generates a project-specific skill directly from analyzed project distinctions. - Path back into current capability / S3: selected/generated skills are persisted under Autohand&#39;s skills boundary and loaded/available to later lead/SubAgent operation; learn update can regenerate stale LLM-generated skills, changing the capability repertoire available to current/future S1 and lead control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in autonomous auto-skill mode the model owns the generated adaptation content that becomes persistent capability; in interactive /learn, the parent operator owns whether to generate/install the adaptation and its scope after model recommendations/gap analysis. - Decision owner: model-backed skill generator/advisor in autonomous base mode; human operator in the distinct parent-governed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProjectAnalyzer; community registry/cache/fetcher; LearnAdvisor; generated-skill metadata/project hash; SkillsRegistry; filesystem persistence/loading; learn/update CLI routing. - Closure path: project/external capability distinctions → model develops adaptation option → autonomous save or parent selection/install → persistent skill enters Autohand registry → subsequent sessions/agents use adapted capability → later project change can trigger re-analysis/regeneration. - Boundary reachability: --auto-skill, /learn, --learn, --learn-update are shipped first-party surfaces at the frozen revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model materially determines the generated specialized capability in the autonomous path; deterministic analyzers/storage record and install the decision. The interactive path moves decisive adoption/generation to the parent and returns that choice into the same persistent capability store. - Evidence: README.md; src/index.ts; src/commands/learn.ts; src/skills/autoSkill.ts; docs/featuremetatools.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic skill extensibility or meta-tool creation alone would not establish S4; the positive state rests on project/external distinctions, capability-option development and persistent adaptation returned into later operation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission modes, policy rules, project/user configuration, role prompts, team limits, tool restrictions and persisted capabilities constrain operation but do not constitute identity/ultimate-policy adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5-level identity or ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established at S5; operator/developer configuration supplies top-level constraints outside a qualifying runtime identity closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission manager/CLI policy, restricted/unrestricted modes, tool filters, agent/skill definitions, team/task limits, hooks/configuration and persisted tools/skills. - Closure path: absent at S5 level; no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing subsequent runtime operation path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can create capabilities and regulate current work, but that does not grant legitimate authority to redefine organizational identity/purpose/ultimate policy; ordinary action/capability approval is not S5. - Evidence: README.md; src/core/toolFilter.ts; configuration/permission/team surfaces at the frozen revision. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers decide future releases and operators can rewrite configuration, but adjacent development/administrative authority is not imported as runtime S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ee588b2ee7bcc139b0ebf2ce234e3cb9545c0043/assessments/autohand-code-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ee588b2ee7bcc139b0ebf2ce234e3cb9545c0043\">ee588b2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"5\" data-a=\"5\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T00:25:33Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gptme/gptme\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gptme</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"5\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>5</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gptme/gptme/tree/0e4ef82bd6a6090bf9cefd357acc8f1dbebf2662\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0e4ef82bd6a6090bf9cefd357acc8f1dbebf2662\">0e4ef82 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended computer work through a model-driven decision/action/observation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user goals, changing workspace and external state, tool results/errors, provider responses, missing information and runtime/context constraints encountered during a task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive response or tool action in light of the current conversation and returned observations. - Decision owner: the autonomous model-driven gptme agent actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: chat.py, tool parsing/dispatch, logs, prompt queue, retries/interrupts, tool allowlists, confirmation hooks, context handling and provider adapters. - Closure path: task/prompt → model reply → model-selected first-party tool invocation → tool result appended to the conversation → next model turn observes the result and revises/continues → completion or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: this is the core shipped gptme runtime across ordinary CLI/server/TUI/Python and non-interactive operation; no development-only actor or external harness is required beyond the configured model provider and environment reached by tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic host code validates and executes actions, but the model owns the substantive next-action choice. Removing the model actor leaves execution machinery without a goal-directed operational decision loop. - Evidence: gptme/chat.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider-side model internals remain outside the repository boundary; the credited first-party ownership is the harness loop around that model cognition.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate filesystem/workspace interference among concurrently delegated first-party child S1 units while preserving a controlled reintegration path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple implementation-capable child agents operating concurrently against one repository could overwrite, expose or otherwise interfere with one another&#39;s in-progress workspace changes. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing first-party gptme child agent loops delegated by the parent in the supported subagent mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent mutation-capable children sharing one repository workspace can collide, overwrite, or expose in-progress changes across operational units. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent selects first-party worktree isolation, giving each selected child a separate Git working tree/branch so its mutations do not directly alter the parent or sibling workspace. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: changed isolated branches and child results return to the parent; the parent can inspect/integrate them before later delegated work, so subsequent S1s operate on the reconciled repository state rather than uncontrolled concurrent mutations. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: credit rests on a concrete cross-S1 filesystem-interference disturbance and the worktree separation/reintegration mechanism that specifically attenuates it, not on fan-out, messaging, routing, or delegation by themselves. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which delegated work should execute in isolated worktrees and how/when returned branches should be inspected or integrated relative to other S1 work. - Decision owner: the autonomous parent gptme agent in the supported subagent-enabled mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolation=&quot;worktree&quot; / isolated execution, per-child workdirs, separate subprocess/thread contexts, smart worktree cleanup, preserved changed branches, completion results and optional parallel/pipeline scheduling. - Closure path: parent identifies/delegates concurrent work → parent selects isolated child execution → first-party runtime creates separate worktree(s), preventing direct workspace mutation/collision → child completes → changed branch/result is returned to parent → parent can inspect/integrate the result before subsequent shared-repository work, changing later S1 operating context. - Boundary reachability: worktree isolation is a documented argument of the shipped built-in subagent tool; the README/CLI expose subagent as a supported selectable tool. The positive path uses first-party gptme children rather than external ACP implementations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: git/worktree machinery deterministically enforces separation, but the parent model has the tool-visible discretion to choose isolated delegation and to act on returned branches. The enforcement layer does not choose the organizational response by itself. - Evidence: gptme/tools/subagent/init.py; gptme/tools/subagent/api.py; gptme/tools/subagent/execution.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: generic parallelism, pipelines, shared budgets and message delivery are not themselves credited as S2. The positive witness is specifically the documented cross-S1 workspace-interference problem and worktree isolation/reintegration relation in the supported multi-agent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a current view of delegated S1 work and exercise present-tense authority over fleet execution, resource bounds and intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child tasks can stall, drift, consume excessive resources, need clarification, complete/fail at different times or require course correction while other work remains active. - Whole-system current view: the parent can list/inspect all registered children and receives first-party progress/completion notifications, status, logs and persisted child metadata across the active delegated fleet. - Current-control decision scope: present-tense spawn/continue/wait/steer/cancel choices plus shared concurrency/token-budget allocation across current child commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which children to spawn/continue, inspect, steer, cancel or await and how to allocate current concurrency/token budget across delegated work. - Decision owner: the autonomous parent gptme agent in subagent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagentlist, status/wait/read-log functions, progress/completion queues, persisted child registry, SubagentBudget, max-concurrency semaphore, timeout/cancel machinery and durable steer/control queues. - Closure path: child status/progress/completion reaches the parent through first-party APIs/hooks → parent model sees the current multi-child state → parent chooses spawn/steer/cancel/wait/continue or budgeted next work → first-party control hooks/semaphores enforce the choice → affected child/fleet operation changes and later state returns to the parent. - Boundary reachability: the complete control surface is packaged with the supported built-in subagent tool and operates with first-party gptme child agents. The tool being opt-in by default is a deployment selection, not a development-only implementation dependency. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrency semaphores, timeout monitors and budget counters enforce limits but do not own current-control discretion. The parent model owns the choice to create/manage/intervene in child commitments using those first-party functions. - Evidence: gptme/tools/subagent/init.py; gptme/tools/subagent/api.py; gptme/tools/subagent/hooks.py; gptme/tools/subagent/types.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the state does not credit a fixed token cap or concurrency limit as S3 ownership. Those are support/enforcement for a separately evidenced parent-agent current-control loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge and verify work produced by another operational agent using a separate audit trajectory with direct access to operational artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementation-producing S1 may report success while code, tests, edge cases, security properties or regression behavior remain wrong or incomplete. - Claim being audited: the producer child&#39;s substantive claim that its implementation/work product is correct, complete and ready for parent acceptance/integration. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing child returns its own complete result/summary and any preserved branch to the parent through the normal subagent completion path. - Complementary access path: a separate verify-role child runs in subprocess + isolated workspace and directly reads the artifacts and can execute local checks/tests through its restricted verifier tool surface. - Independence boundary: the verifier is a distinct model trajectory/process with fresh child context, an isolated worktree and verifier-specific tool/profile constraints rather than the producing child re-reading its own report. - Who acts on findings: the autonomous parent gptme agent receives the verifier completion through the first-party completion hook and can reject, retry, steer, re-delegate or integrate subsequent work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make the substantive audit judgment about whether the producer&#39;s work withstands direct independent inspection/checking and report findings back for correction or acceptance. - Decision owner: the autonomous verifier gptme child agent; the parent agent owns the subsequent current-control response to the audit result. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in verifier profile, role=&quot;verify&quot; defaults, subprocess execution, isolated worktree, fresh child context, verifier tool restrictions, direct read/shell/ipython access, complete result and parent completion hook. Attestation hashes/provenance can support evidence integrity but are not treated as the audit decision owner. - Closure path: producing child/work creates an artifact or claim → parent invokes a verify-role child → first-party role resolution runs verifier in a separate subprocess and isolated workspace → verifier directly reads/runs checks against operational artifacts rather than relying only on producer summary → verifier model returns its judgment via complete → first-party completion hook places the result in the parent conversation → parent can steer/retry/reject/integrate subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: verifier role/profile, subprocess isolation, worktree creation and completion return are all shipped first-party components of the supported subagent mode; no external evaluator service or test-only actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: isolation and tool allowlists create independence, but the verifier model itself performs the discretionary audit judgment. The parent then receives the judgment through a first-party feedback channel. This is not merely deterministic verdict parsing or self-verification by the producer. - Evidence: gptme/tools/subagent/types.py; gptme/profiles.py; gptme/tools/subagent/api.py; gptme/tools/subagent/execution.py; gptme/tools/subagent/hooks.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a generic second model opinion or the standalone attestation utility would not be sufficient. Credit rests on the packaged verifier role&#39;s distinct process/workspace/tool access plus returned audit judgment and parent corrective path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert newly learned future-relevant distinctions from present work into durable context that alters later-session capability/behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: later sessions would otherwise repeat discovery of user preferences, project facts, decisions/rationales and recurring problem-solving context learned from prior interaction with the environment. - External distinction: a user preference, project/environment fact, decision rationale or recurring problem pattern encountered through present interaction/tool work and judged relevant beyond the current turn. - Future / prospective distinction: the agent explicitly evaluates whether that newly learned distinction is worth remembering for future sessions rather than only preserving same-task working context. - Adaptation option generated: the model formulates a named durable memory containing the selected reusable distinction for later-session use. - Path back into current capability / S3: first-party memory storage/indexing persists the option and later promptworkspace construction automatically injects persistent memory into subsequent sessions, changing the context available to future operational/current-control decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which learned distinction is worth preserving for future work and formulate the durable memory content. - Decision owner: the autonomous gptme model using the built-in memory tool in an autonomous/non-interactive mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory ToolSpec and memory store/index, layered memory roots, future-session promptworkspace loading, confirmation hooks and the autonomous autoconfirm hook. - Closure path: current S1 encounters a future-relevant user/project/environment distinction → model judges it worth remembering and calls memory with the selected content → first-party runtime persists/indexes the memory → a later gptme session automatically loads persistent-memory indexes/content into system context → later model decisions operate with the returned distinction, changing subsequent capability/behavior. - Boundary reachability: memory is a built-in first-party tool path, and the repository ships an explicit autonomous/non-interactive auto-confirm hook that confirms tool execution without human interaction. The future read path is in first-party workspace prompt construction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: storage/index/prompt injection are deterministic support. The model owns the prospective adaptation judgment — what newly learned distinction merits durable reuse and what content to preserve. Interactive confirmation can constrain a separate operator mode, but the supported autonomous mode closes without a human adaptation owner. - Evidence: gptme/tools/memory.py; gptme/prompts/workspace.py; gptme/hooks/autoconfirm.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: context compaction, pre-authored lessons and profile-global memory by themselves are not credited as S4. The positive witness is the model-owned learn→select→persist→future automatic reuse loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: gptme has prompts, profiles, tool permissions, confirmation/guardrail hooks and operator configuration, but no shipped loop was found in which an identity-level or ultimate-policy matter is autonomously or parent-governedly deliberated, authoritatively decided and returned as governing policy for the organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system/agent instruction files, profiles, tool allowlists, confirmation hooks, behavior rules, environment/project configuration and operator-selected runtime modes. - Closure path: these constraints can shape subsequent execution, but they enter as configured/static rules or ordinary operational approvals rather than an identity/ultimate-policy matter flowing to an authoritative S5 decision and back into organization-wide policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces supplied constraints and the model follows prompt/profile policy, but neither enforcement nor compliance establishes the missing ultimate-policy decision function. Ordinary confirmation of a tool or memory write is not S5. - Evidence: gptme/profiles.py; gptme/tools/init.py; gptme/hooks/cliconfirm.py; gptme/prompts/workspace.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users/organizations embedding gptme may supply constitutional governance externally; that higher-recursion authority is not imported into the repository-relative assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cf806bc3e98e58562ac7e513aa6492edb40693f5/assessments/gptme.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cf806bc3e98e58562ac7e513aa6492edb40693f5\">cf806bc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T20:51:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewiKim/autoresearchlab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoResearchLab</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewiKim/autoresearchlab/tree/bd70a67aafa172a4e7c5a3f4477d6e438cfeb0d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bd70a67aafa172a4e7c5a3f4477d6e438cfeb0d8\">bd70a67 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous role lanes produce scoped scientific work: locate and evaluate evidence, design a falsifiable experiment, provision execution, run analysis, and write defensible results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar academic domains, unavailable tools/datasets, conflicting data quality, unanticipated engineering failures, analytical alternatives and result uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Scout agents choose/refine field-specific queries, Analyst chooses estimands and tests, Engineer chooses how to provision the selected toolchain without altering the science, and Runner writes/runs the contract-bound analysis; their outputs determine subsequent experimental work. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed role agents operating through the first-party lane contracts; external Codex CLI executes the model sessions but its mere availability does not establish additional first-party S-functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust lane dispatch, role-scoped sandboxes, sealed contracts/artifacts, runtime state and deterministic validity checks. - Closure path: Role plan → agent work/result artifact → contract and gate checks → next role uses the output or reports a blocker to the director → subsequent experiment/review changes accordingly. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI and agent plan construct these Codex role lanes, the native harness runs them and persists returned results; these are not example-only or CI-only role stubs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents possess bounded scientific and implementation discretion; queue/budget enforcement and filesystem persistence are deterministic supports, not the source of their scientific choices. - Evidence: role rights; reasoning roles in shipped plan; native lane entry. - Basis: Structural shipped agent-role/decision and result-return paths, supplemented by explicit source-owning architecture descriptions. - Confidence: High for first-party configurability and decision ownership, not a measurement of model effectiveness. - Caveats: No live Codex/LLM agent, scholarly API or actual research experiment was run in this assessment; science quality is not a VSM autonomy grade.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate stabilization of cross-role experiment/review work, but a separately established inter-S1 oscillation-attenuation function remains ambiguous at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing author experiment requests, engineering/analysis handoff dependencies, status messages crowding other channels and duplicate/superseded work may disrupt delivery to other scientific units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The bus carries author→director requests and enforces per-channel capacity, unique identity, delivery receipts and escalation; LabDirector can adopt/decline experiments with reasons. It is not established that the latter is a distinct inter-S1 damping decision rather than S3 prioritization and sequential task handoff. - Decision owner: Agents answer substantive requests, but deterministic bus/scheduler implements capacity, escalation and delivery; S2-specific ownership is unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Request/standing channel registry, per-channel quotas, stateful deduplication, escalations, lane sequencing and contract-bound handoffs. - Closure path: Author request → bus → director response → changed experiment/document plan is plausible, but the reviewed evidence does not separate this from hierarchical current-control return sufficiently for an autonomous S2 claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual feedback messages and decisions exist; their S2-specific function and direct reciprocal stabilization of distinct operational units cannot be inferred merely from those mechanisms. - Evidence: author request with owed reason; per-channel capacity and delivery; projection and return; documented channel history. - Basis: Structural coordination candidates; unknown function-specific inter-S1 relation. - Confidence: Medium that coordination machinery exists; low on attribution of a separate S2 function. - Caveats: The specific unresolved witness is two independently operating S1 cells whose mutual interference, rather than a parent backlog/approval dependency, is attenuated through an accountable, closed S2 path.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate present scientific commitments and next work for the entire current research program, responding to failed reviews, stalled runs, evidence gaps and competing follow-up demands. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Expensive repeated analyses, weak lead hypotheses, unresolved gate blockers, scientific stagnation, contradictory handoffs and limited lane budget/time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: LabDirector selects/refines/pivots/concludes a research lead and chooses follow-up scientific tasks, based on Chronicle, current gate results and supervisor notices; it may not override independently recorded reviewer scores. - Decision owner: The model-backed LabDirector agent for scientific priority/correction. Supervisor stall detection, Rust dispatch/termination, budgets and hard rubric gates constrain or request action but do not own that scientific decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cycle-wide summaries and notes, request escalations, lane priority/dispatch, budget and stagnation guards, protected lead memory, append-only feedback. - Closure path: Whole-cycle evidence/active review blocker → director&#39;s scientific correction/pivot/priority decision → role task/lead updates → scheduler launches affected subsequent lanes → newer results are reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: The default agent plan includes a LabDirector across stages, and the native cycle uses its lead decisions and reviewer feedback to create follow-ups; not a maintainer-only or external governance workflow. - Whole-system current view: Chronicle digest, evidence/decision notes, active blockers, gate review and lane/cycle states across the same research-program boundary. - Current-control decision scope: Next research work, which lead/claim is current, which further measurements or corrections are owed, and whether to pivot or conclude under the fixed scientific and resource constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The director has substantive authority to adjudicate scientific commitments; a deterministic forced-stall request cannot replace its selection of a scientifically justified next hypothesis. - Evidence: director role and limits; agent plan duty; active blocker review return; source-bound strategy decisions. - Basis: Explicit role contract plus structural gate→decision→followup path. - Confidence: Medium-high for source-wired current control; no live effectiveness evidence. - Caveats: Forced livelock termination and resource enforcement are deterministic. Owner instructions can constrain individual choices, but ordinary operator steering does not by itself establish a distinct closed S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge the producing research roles&#39; hypothesis, validity, technical/reproducibility and reporting claims before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novelty overclaims, unsound analysis, stale evidence, unreplicated results, and a production lane&#39;s optimistic interpretation of its own outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately dispatched Gatekeeper model agents assess current bound evidence and return approve/challenge/reject, rubric scores and missing validations; their judgments cannot be overwritten by LabDirector. - Decision owner: Independent Gatekeeper agent lanes; deterministic rubric parsing, conjunctive gates and scheduling enforce the returned review, without claiming autonomous judgment for the scorer code itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate lane state, current-lead/validation binding, reviewer role split, sealed panel reports, structural rubric gates and append-only review history. - Closure path: Producing roles&#39; claim and validation artifacts → separate reviewer access/judgment → gate objections and rejection persisted → LabDirector receives binding scientific blockers and chooses changed investigation/followup → subsequent review of revised evidence. - Boundary reachability: Gatekeepers are included in the normal agent plan and their result parsing/aggregation is wired into the production review path; this is not CI testing or a developer-only quality script. - Claim being audited: Research lead&#39;s purported novelty, technical validity, reproducibility, measured results and completeness of evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Analyst/Runner outputs and LabDirector/Chronicle research summary. - Complementary access path: Separate gatekeeper prompts bound to the current lead and validation artifacts, literature graph and prior reviews; independent rubric outputs and objections. - Independence boundary: Distinct role/lanes, sealed review collection and prohibition on director score override, though all may use the same external model family; independence is organizational/role, not proof of model independence or external peer review. - Who acts on findings: LabDirector for scientific replanning; native gate enforces publication/promotion hold until sufficient review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed reviewers choose scientific objections and verdicts with their own role contracts. The JS aggregator applies those decisions but does not generate the underlying audit judgment. - Evidence: separation of powers; independent lane design; gatekeeper plan; review parsing and provenance; review objections/return. - Basis: Explicit source boundary plus executable reviewer-lane/result-return structure. - Confidence: Medium-high on first-party complementary audit mode, not on external peer-review quality. - Caveats: A Gatekeeper may see summaries rather than independently replaying an experiment; S3 positive is narrowly complementary access through separate role, literature/review evidence and scientific corrective return, not a claim of adversarial execution of the raw dataset on every run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Model a changing scientific field and generate future research-agenda options from its beliefs, gaps and frontier, returning selected options into the current program&#39;s experimental capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New competing hypotheses and publications, oversaturated or already-solved research questions, uncertainty about future impact and which experimental directions deserve commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent-owned Scout/LabDirector field/belief/candidate synthesis and independent expert comparisons develop future-facing research options. A supported timeout mode automatically promotes the highest eligible ranked survivor; a distinct normal mode lets the researcher choose from the same evidence-grounded candidate slate, after which LabDirector operationalizes the choice. - Decision owner: Autonomous research agents own the external interpretation, slate and expert judgments in the base mode; the legitimate cycle-owning human explicitly owns the final agenda choice in the supported selection mode. Rust gate merely enforces the chosen candidate and resumes work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Field landscape quality gate, SOTA cards, instrument-blind candidate slate, sealed expert panel, novelty refutation, feasibility anchors, owner pause/selection file and timeout policy. - Closure path: Field literature/frontier → SOTA/beliefs → agent-created candidate futures → panel and novelty/feasibility filtering → automatic survivor or parent-selected candidate → LabDirector lead and reproduction anchor → current Analyst/Engineer/Runner work changes. - Boundary reachability: Expert selection is dispatched by the first-party native program when a lead is absent and its landscape/cards are ready; the published owner pause and timeout are supported runtime paths, not research-paper suggestions. - External distinction: Newly examined scientific claims, field-wide paper frontiers, contradictions, open problems and external assets in the requested research field. - Future / prospective distinction: A ranked set of not-yet-undertaken research programs designed to change accepted beliefs, evaluated for novelty, uncertainty and feasible reproduction. - Adaptation option generated: Alternative research hypotheses and experiment programs, including cross-field/bottleneck paths; the selected candidate changes the research lab&#39;s lead and required future experiment capabilities. - Path back into current capability / S3: The selected-vetted candidate is supplied as the bound new lead and next reproduction work in the director&#39;s current-control path, constraining subsequent role task creation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent roles make the field/option judgments, with an operational automatic option; separately the user has a genuine alternative supported decision right, so (P) is not inferred merely from a configuration knob. - Evidence: source landscape selection engagement; instrument-blind slate; owner choice return to lead; runtime owner checkpoint; timeout selection. - Basis: Structural source paths for field-informed future agenda and two alternative supported selection owners. - Confidence: Medium for S4 ownership boundary; high for the existence of selection paths. - Caveats: This is prospective adaptation of a research program&#39;s agenda, not proven autonomous redesign of AutoResearchLab&#39;s own software, model weights or deployment. Whether the optional timeout is enabled is configuration-dependent; no live literature search or model evaluation was run.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separately established first-party ultimate identity/policy arbitration with a closed decision-return path at the research-program recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User research directions and binding owner directives affect venue, method count, project preferences and evidentiary discipline, but these are program/task priorities and source-quality constraints rather than a demonstrated ultimate-policy dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The owner may submit a research request, select the lead, and record binding instructions with reasons. No shipped role or explicit parent path adjudicates a conflict over the institution&#39;s identity/ultimate policy as distinct from research-agenda/current-control decisions. - Decision owner: External research owner supplies task/domain/program preferences; validity controls enforce them without acquiring ultimate-policy discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research request/configuration, owner-directive file, role prompt injection, scientific score rules and selected-candidate enforcement. - Closure path: Owner research instructions → role task constraints and agenda decisions. Missing identity-level issue → legitimate policy deliberation/decision → governing policy returned to subsequent program operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A binding preference and research agenda selection are material parent decisions, but not automatically S5. A static instruction or scientific gate also cannot own ultimate policy merely by enforcement. - Evidence: owner directives and scope; binding role clause; owner research selection. - Basis: Bounded source-first negative after distinguishing parent agenda and scientific control from true ultimate-policy authority. - Confidence: Medium-high within the supported single research-program boundary. - Caveats: A human university or research institution can exercise a higher-recursion S5 outside the software; no organizational parent mode is imported from its mere possible presence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8610c1721397b1fd22b2b433c18fbc1e01311151/assessments/au/autoresearchlab.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8610c1721397b1fd22b2b433c18fbc1e01311151\">8610c17 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:04:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/roloVibes/Halo-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Halo Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/roloVibes/Halo-Harness/tree/710dde651035be3f204598aee90a1e4b85e3d6e8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 710dde651035be3f204598aee90a1e4b85e3d6e8\">710dde6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute model-directed workspace tasks and operational worker lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/research work, tool failures, provider/context limits and incomplete artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next tool, code/research action or response from feedback. - Decision owner: The first-party session/worker model actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python model/tool loop, provider transports, permission engine, local tools/MCP, sessions, role lineups and worktrees. - Closure path: Model request → selected tools → observations/session state → subsequent model step or worker result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Halo owns its loop rather than merely configuring Claude Code. Claude-compatible settings and external model endpoints do not donate control functions. - Evidence: haloharness/agent/loop.py; haloharness/agent/subagent.py; haloharness/orchestra/workers.py; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Normal halo CLI/TUI and Orchestra adapters instantiate the native session/worker loop; installed role/model configuration does not require a third-party agent runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate overlapping file-unit writes and merge conflicts across distinct Orchestra worker lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agents may claim overlapping functions/file regions or produce mutually conflicting changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Workers choose a different claim, wait or escalate on refusal; a fresh judge selects a conflict resolution when branches collide. - Decision owner: Worker/lead model actors and the fresh conflict-judge model own the discretionary choices; code owns atomic admission and merge/test enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AST/heading/file-unit claim keys, exclusive claim log, guarded writes, per-lane worktrees, serialized merges, test checks and bounded send-back. - Closure path: Claim refusal returns holder/expiry to worker → changed claim/wait/escalation; conflicting branches → fresh judge sees goal/hunks → proposed text applied/tested → failed resolution undone and piece returned to the worker for revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A is grounded in model-owned response to a concrete cross-S1 disturbance, particularly the fresh conflict resolution and worker correction, not in deterministic locks or sequence alone. - Evidence: docs/ORCHESTRA.md; haloharness/orchestra/claims.py; haloharness/tools/orchestraclaims.py; haloharness/orchestra/merge.py; haloharness/orchestra/adapters.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Orchestra run creates real native worker sessions and exposes claim tools/guard in their run environment; merge uses the shipped fresh-judge adapter.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate whole-run current resources and commitments after forecast/return/block/stall drift. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Projected overspend, repeated returns, blocked dependencies/claims and stalled lanes threaten the current goal organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose revised model/bio, split remaining work, add a read-only specialist, retry with corrective context or stop; in review mode the owner decides whether to apply the proposed revision. - Decision owner: Auto mode: the lead model owns the checked replan. Review/parked budget mode: the legitimate local operator owns the returned acceptance/cap decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Drift thresholds, current lane/spend/forecast snapshots, plan validation, immutable merged-lane protection, pause/checkpoint, durable decision rows and deterministic apply/resume. - Closure path: Drift → lanes pause → lead receives whole remaining plan/current evidence → auto applies a valid agent plan, or review parks its diff → operator approves/rejects/adjusts cap → split/claims/workflow/forecast updated and remaining operation resumes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead makes the substantive current-control revision in auto mode; validation and budgets enforce it. Review mode separately returns an operator decision into operation. Neither hard stops nor initial task splitting alone supplies the positive witness. - Evidence: docs/SUPERVISE.md; haloharness/supervise/drift.py; haloharness/supervise/replanask.py; haloharness/supervise/replanapply.py; haloharness/supervise/decisions.py; haloharness/supervise/apply.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Optional supervised modes; replan limits/cooldown apply, completed lanes are pinned, workflow-pinned stage sets disable free replanning, and budget ceilings remain separately operator-owned.. - Boundary reachability: halo supervise/goal options expose replan:auto and replan:review; ordinary runtime adapters call the lead and apply checked plans. Attention answers are polled and applied to the same queued goal.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement production claims with raw-code multi-dimension review, independent finding adjudication and correction/re-verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Author summaries or reviewer allegations may be wrong; a claimed fix may leave the defect intact. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer finds code defects; independent judge confirms/dismisses against source; confirmed findings are corrected by a separate fixer and judged again against the new code. - Decision owner: Fresh read-only reviewer/judge model actors own audit judgments; a coder actor owns scoped correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in dimension prompts, fixed target/diff resolution, read-only agent specs omitting CLAUDE.md, structured verdicts, serial fixes, file hashes and current-source re-verification. - Closure path: halo review target --fix → raw artifact review → judge traces claim → CONFIRMED finding goes to fixer → changed file hash/current diff → judge rechecks → fixed only when the defect is dismissed; failed/unparseable checks remain PLAUSIBLE/skipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The separate code-inspecting actors add complementary reality access and a shipped corrective return. Deterministic Orchestra gates and model tuning statistics are not promoted to audit ownership. - Evidence: docs/REVIEW.md; haloharness/review/run.py; haloharness/review/agents.py; haloharness/review/verify.py; haloharness/review/fix.py; haloharness/review/prompts/judge.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Report-only mode alone is not claimed to complete correction. The positive witness includes --fix with verification; review exit code 0 is not evidence of zero findings or approval.. - Boundary reachability: halo review and /review call the common built-in runreview; --fix is a shipped option, not an unwired developer callback, and it requires verification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Develop prospective model-roster capability adaptations from external ecosystem options and local operating constraints, with operator selection returned into the roster/roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing embedding/vision/role capabilities, model-family blind-spot overlap and newly available model families can leave future tasks unsupported or costly. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether to add/verify a new external model, retire an overlap or repoint a role after inspecting capability/cost/fit options. - Decision owner: The legitimate self-hosting operator; deterministic adviser/battery/UI supply options and enforcement, not an autonomous S4 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: External Ollama/Hugging Face library/catalog metadata and dated snapshot, local host memory/capability profiles, gap/freshness/mismatch rules, ranked options with download/VRAM/role impact, download-and-verify job and explicit role acceptance. - Closure path: External/local roster distinctions → bounded ranked adaptation options → operator selects Download and verify/retire/point → model pulled, battery profile written and role offered → accepted assignment updates the active lineup/role for subsequent work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: P is supported by a function-specific compare/select/apply path. Neither deterministic recommendation nor ordinary current model routing is called autonomous S4; the operator owns the prospective capability choice. - Evidence: docs/MODELS-adviser.md; docs/MODELS-ollama.md; haloharness/modelslibrary/advise.py; haloharness/modelslibrary/adviserules.py; haloharness/modelslibrary/browse.py; haloharness/studio/localsearchapi.py; haloharness/studio/localverifyjob.py; haloharness/studio/rolespointapi.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: The adviser is deterministic and the packaged ecosystem snapshot can be stale; freshness is not measured superiority. No live download, benchmark or successful capability gain is claimed by this source assessment.. - Boundary reachability: Official halo local advise/Studio Local Models/Library exposes suggestions and the same supported verify/assign/role-point routes; this is installed product behavior, not a research plan.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy arbitration function is supplied at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Goal stop criteria, permissions, fleet control grants, role bios and deployment policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific identity/ultimate-policy decisive return path established. - Decision owner: Operator/configuration owns constraints; no qualifying S5 owner/path established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WHAT/DONE/STOP goal fields, role/lineup configuration, approval and fleet control levels, supervised plan/budget decisions and local settings. - Closure path: Ordinary goal/resource/permission decisions return into work, but no distinct identity-level arbitration/return is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These paths close S1/S3 decisions or security constraints. Renaming them policy or giving a user final operational say does not establish S5. - Evidence: docs/ORCHESTRA.md; docs/SUPERVISE.md; docs/FLEET.md; docs/TEAM.md; haloharness/permissions.py; haloharness/supervise/decisions.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f6ad0072b8a1021a3d4bb4675ec159f4d8488d4/assessments/ha/halo-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f6ad0072b8a1021a3d4bb4675ec159f4d8488d4\">9f6ad00 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:48:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davejj1530/openorc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenOrc</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davejj1530/openorc/tree/85bf1c5e4c81000911d8a5a90e23dbb7cf869283\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 85bf1c5e4c81000911d8a5a90e23dbb7cf869283\">85bf1c5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce software/task results with durable first-party role/action/feedback composition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous tasks, code/tool failures, delegation outcomes and changing user direction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model members choose edits, delegated work, messages and terminal results through scoped MCP tools. - Decision owner: installed Codex/Claude/OpenCode actors explicitly assembled into first-party run/team roles; external CLI internals are not separately credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, RunHandle, task briefs, MCP scope, worktrees, snapshots, journals and settlement/recovery. - Closure path: user/task brief → provider model action → local tools/files/child result → resumed model turn → captured result and reported completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive work choices come from model actors; host state transitions only transport/enforce them. - Caveats: requires installed authenticated CLI; source topology is not live model quality. - Boundary reachability: desktop/core constructs provider adapters and the scoped MCP server; team instructions are injected into actual actor turns, whose tool calls mutate the same live journal and return results. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/run-launch.ts; packages/core/src/services/run-turns.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-instructions.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-assignments.ts; packages/core/src/openorc.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-file and assignment interference through agent-owned claim negotiation and returned correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent coding members may edit the same shared file, duplicate ownership or apply incompatible user direction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: members choose file ownership/release, alternative work or directed negotiation; lead/members relay concrete corrections to affected workers. - Decision owner: model-backed team members/lead own coordination choices; journal claims and refusal are enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teamclaim, teamsay/teammessage, active claims, role boundaries, captured changed files and isolated assignment integration. - Closure path: claim conflict returns holder/file → model asks colleague or selects other work → colleague releases/revises scope → later claim/work proceeds; affected active workers receive new direction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the conflict response; a fixed refusal alone is not the credited autonomous owner. - Caveats: claims are advisory and depend on model compliance; not an OS-level write sandbox, and live effectiveness is unmeasured. - Boundary reachability: Beta team execution exposes teamclaim and chat tools to running members, with conflict text returned directly to the requesting model. - Distinct S1 units: distinct coding members/contribution actors producing changes in a common project workspace or isolated child assignments. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file ownership and incompatible concurrent edits/direction, explicitly addressed by first-party shared-workspace instructions and held-path refusal. - Attenuating coordination relation: claim before editing; on collision ask current holder in chat or choose another file; release claims and deliver scoped corrections to affected members. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: holder/conflict data and chat are included in subsequent actor turns, changing who edits which files and under which direction. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this witness names the shared-file collision, actor-selected response and resumed work; the generic message bus and hierarchy alone are not counted. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/team-instructions.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-chat.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-delivery.ts; packages/core/src/mcp-host/team.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent current-control over the whole active local team execution and its operational commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active assignments may need stopping/recovery, changed direction or intervention after conflicting/failed integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator inspects whole-team runtime and selects scoped direction, stop, actor retry, Plan/Act implementation and retained integration recovery. - Decision owner: legitimate local desktop user owns this supervisory current-control mode; lead performs delegated work coordination; fixed scheduler does not own resource discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime actor/attempt/task view, ownership checks, direction/admission keys, scoped delivery, stop/retry and guarded recovery receipts. - Closure path: whole-team runtime → operator intervention/direction → coordinator fences/stops/retries or delivers changed direction → actors resume or withhold work under the returned decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: P is based on the combined team-wide view and applied intervention path, not a generic stop button or the class name TeamCoordinator. No autonomous whole-system S3 choice is established. - Caveats: scope is one local team/project execution, not all organizations or a global fleet. User must exercise the supported control; source establishes capability, not observed intervention history. - Boundary reachability: desktop orchestration RPC exposes current team runtime and guarded send/stop/retry/implementPlan/recovery methods; they act on the owned execution journal rather than an adjacent dashboard. - Whole-system current view: team conversation runtime includes pinned team revision, execution actors/attempts, retained work/results and recovery state across active member cells. - Current-control decision scope: current team commitment/direction, withholding or restarting affected actors, moving planning to implementation and applying retained integration recovery; scheduler limits only enforce the selected envelope. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/team-conversation.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-coordinator.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-attempt-recovery.ts; packages/core/src/handlers/orchestration.ts. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent source-backed model review challenges proposed code before user publication or corrective implementation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported completion claims and defects in proposed PR code; previous findings may remain after revision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate review model chooses findings and summary against raw checkout/diff; user retains posting and follow-through selection. - Decision owner: dedicated review conversation/model owns audit judgment; Git checkout/draft parser and human publication are distinct support/control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: detached PR checkout, Plan-mode review, scoped finding/summary tools, exact-commit drafts and re-review after head change. - Closure path: raw PR code/diff → separate reviewer model findings → draft visible to operator/control; selected findings can be sent into task/team implementation and later review checks whether addressed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit judgment is model-authored, not the coder completion narrative or deterministic Git diff. Human ownership of external posting does not author the audit conclusion. - Caveats: not independent provider validation or measured detection efficacy; arbitrary team member named reviewer alone is not credited. - Boundary reachability: the shipped PR-review service creates the dedicated conversation/checkout, injects review-only instructions and exposes draft finding tools; normal task/team review selections have first-party return paths. - Claim being audited: proposed source changes are correct and prior findings have been addressed. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation conversation, task result and PR description. - Complementary access path: detached current-head source checkout plus direct diff, with subsequent reviews comparing changed commits rather than merely repeating author reports. - Independence boundary: separate review conversation in Plan mode with explicit prohibition on implementation, file changes, push and posting; isolated from the author workspace. - Who acts on findings: operator sees/selects draft findings; task/team review-send paths return selected corrections to implementation; subsequent model review checks retained findings against new code. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/pull-requests.ts; packages/core/src/services/pull-request-checkout.ts; packages/core/src/services/review.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-tasks.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no outside-and-future adaptation loop into OpenOrc current capability established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task context, project lessons, user preferences and updated software versions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: memory extraction and instruction changes retain collaboration context; updater checks releases. - Decision owner: task/Orcling agents own local content changes; operator and maintainers own externally selected capability/product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project memory/retrieval, Orcling histories/instructions, model selection and release updater. - Closure path: past work/user preference is reused or a released app is updated; no prospective environmental model develops adaptation options and returns a capability decision into current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, self-instruction edits and update notification do not by themselves satisfy S4. - Caveats: does not rule out a larger organization using outputs for strategy. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/memory.ts; packages/core/src/services/orcling-briefs.ts; packages/core/src/services/orclings.ts; apps/desktop/src/main/app-updates.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain a persistent Orcling behavioral identity/charter in alternative agent-owned and parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new lasting collaboration requirements or owner dissatisfaction require changing the same actor instructions across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allow mode lets the Orcling choose and save revised enduring instructions; Approve/Plan requests explicit parent authorization, and user profile editing/restoration supplies a direct parent mode. - Decision owner: Orcling model for autonomous instruction revision; local user for approved/editor/restore mode and immutable permission/identity ceilings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned orclinginstructions, reason/author records, action-policy approval gate, profile editing/restoration and fresh-process identity brief. - Closure path: lasting identity/working rule → model revised charter or user-selected edit/restore → version saved → next session/process injects current instructions wherever the actor works. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous base is the local actor charter, not authority to widen host permissions; parent mode separately owns identity/permission selection and approved charter change. - Caveats: not every instruction edit is S5: the positive witness concerns persistent identity/behavioral mandate. Models remain under parent-set host permission ceilings; no global institutional S5 is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Orcling tools are mounted for the saved speaker; updateInstructions authorizes according to its effective mode and saves a version. New provider processes receive the same saved identity/current charter through orclingBrief. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the long-term companion role and enduring behavioral instructions that define how this actor works across projects and conversations. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Allow mode delegates charter revision to the actor within its immutable host ceiling; Approve/Plan and user edit/restore retain parent decision over the proposed charter/identity. - Return-to-operation path: saveInstructions/restoreInstructions append the authoritative version; orclingBrief puts current instructions into each newly started process, with an explicit next-session boundary. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/orcling-briefs.ts; packages/core/src/services/orclings.ts; packages/core/src/services/app-actions.ts; packages/core/src/services/run-approvals.ts; packages/core/src/mcp-host/orcling.ts; docs/orclings.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4215303042bfc9da6b6d818d0713d0fe9f20001/assessments/op/openorc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4215303042bfc9da6b6d818d0713d0fe9f20001\">a421530 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T12:53:31Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ferrule</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule/tree/24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\">24023de &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding/assistant/scheduled outcomes through a model/tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific files, commands, service responses, tool failures and verification errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select actions, tool arguments and continuation after observations. - Decision owner: root and child model-backed agents within their assigned outcomes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, sandbox/credential proxy, verification, transcript, scheduler and budget/stuck guards. - Closure path: call → tools → observations → repeated model choice; returned results and failed check tails enter subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: shipped run/chat/gateway and child factories construct ferrulecore::Agent and equip product tools; no external cognitive harness is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement alone cannot choose the next task-dependent action if the model is removed. - Evidence: shipped runtime; core loop entry; action/verification loop; child construction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent duplicate task effort and mutually destructive sibling workspace edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two children attempt the same work or modify the same parent checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/claim a non-conflicting task, select worktree isolation and decide when to integrate a returned branch. - Decision owner: participating model agents, with the root owning integration into its checkout. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic SQLite claims/dependency refusals, isolated worktrees and returned branch/workspace notes. - Closure path: claim refusal returns current ownership/dependency information; agents select other work or wait; worker changes remain isolated until root merge/cherry-pick. - Boundary reachability: the supplied tasks and spawnagent tools are registered for the actual running tree, not hypothetical external coordination code. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent root/worker/planner agents producing separate code/work outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate task claims and concurrent code edits contaminating one another. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive task claim plus per-worker worktree and root-controlled integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: claim/result/branch metadata changes what work an agent can take and which changes enter subsequent parent operation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: atomic task ownership and workspace separation directly regulate identified duplicate-effort/edit collisions; the shared board alone is not the witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents choose tasks/isolation and root integration; transactions/git merely enforce those choices. - Evidence: task choice/feedback contract; atomic conflict-aware claim; tool dispatch; isolation wiring; worktree integration boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Outside git or on worktree failure a child may share the workspace; this is reported, not claimed equally isolated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current child commitments/capacity and deployment spending on behalf of the root tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many live commitments, shared token scarcity and children needing intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root selects wait/message/stop and renewed child effort; parent selects shared caps and approved administrative intervention. - Decision owner: root agent in the base tree-control mode; legitimate self-hosting operator in the separate parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status/token projection, budget warnings, stop flags/tree accounting, admin approval cards, shared Hub caps and audit/config persistence. - Closure path: root interventions change running child behaviour or stop descendants; approved parent cap changes are applied to the live Hub and subsequent runs are governed by them. - Boundary reachability: messageagent/stopagent are actual root tools; owner chat ferruleadmin and dashboard use the same first-party control APIs. No adjacent benchmark supervisor is credited. - Whole-system current view: root lists its current children with role/status/token use and reports, and receives tree budget feedback; operator sees gateway health/current turns, tasks and shared caps. - Current-control decision scope: current operational commitments and scarce tree/deployment resources, beyond merely decomposing the user&#39;s task. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root chooses intervention under current operational feedback; fixed max-depth/token checks do not own that choice. In parent mode the operator&#39;s exact approval/cap decision owns the intervention. - Evidence: current child view and intervention tools; current consumption view; tree budget feedback; parent health/current control; operator status view; live cap return; approval execution. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A is scoped to root-tree current commitments; it is not an autonomous governor of all gateway roots or OSS governance. The P mode is independently supported, not simultaneous dual ownership.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: optionally challenge a worker/parent claim or code change through a separate verifier&#39;s raw-artifact access. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a plausible completion report may hide broken tests, incorrect code or contaminated evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifier chooses proof commands/code inspection and judges whether the submitted claim holds. - Decision owner: a non-forked verifier agent in a separate session; parent acts on returned findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt/role, throwaway workspace snapshot, separate transcript, optional distinct provider and parent notice/report return. - Closure path: verifier result is delivered to the parent, which can reject the claim, redirect/restart work or inspect further through its existing message/stop/tool paths. - Boundary reachability: spawnagent role=verifier is a shipped runtime mode with role-specific placement and child construction, not a test/eval-only role. - Claim being audited: a submitted operational completion claim or code change. - Ordinary reporting path: worker final report/status to parent. - Complementary access path: verifier directly reads the copied code including uncommitted/new files and runs proof commands. - Independence boundary: separate non-forked model context and snapshot; verifier does not implement/repair production changes. Same provider is allowed but is not claimed as model independence. - Who acts on findings: the root/parent with authority to redirect or stop the relevant child and change its own next operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the evidence-selection/verdict, rather than a deterministic parser or self-reported success flag. - Evidence: audit role purpose; snapshot placement; fresh child context/role; return and parent response modes; verifier workspace boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional claim-relative audit mode, not every verifier invocation. Forking producer context, using it as ordinary same-path QA, or choosing non-isolated shared work can invalidate this independence claim; no live audit or general reliability guarantee is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied outside-and-future adaptation process with the required supervisory coupling established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future external provider/tool/user changes could demand capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped future-relevant option judgment and two-way S3 adoption loop established. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: offline reflection/probing/playbook deltas, model discovery/testing/fallbacks, doctor and install/update/admin operations. - Closure path: learning applies lessons to later prompts and admin changes current capability; the inspected triggers are retrospective internal failures or present configuration needs, without an explicit external/prospective adaptation distinction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning between runs and autonomous self-configuration are not sufficient S4 evidence. - Evidence: retrospective learning inputs; learning implementation; contrastive pass scope; admin change scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain legitimate ultimate authority over the deployment&#39;s autonomy/security envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: whether Ferrule may act without per-change consent while protected keys and owner-only boundaries remain reserved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the operator chooses Ask me first versus Protected autonomous and controls security settings that the bot cannot alter. - Decision owner: the authenticated deployment owner via the dashboard; the model cannot grant itself this authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: page-only sandbox-mode API, confirmation/fresh-config guard, protected configuration keys, audit record, saved policy and gateway restart. - Closure path: the owner&#39;s mode choice is saved and audited; the gateway restarts with the returned sandbox/trust envelope, changing subsequent admin/tool behaviour. - Boundary reachability: dashboard sandboxmode is a first-party operating endpoint explicitly omitted from chat operation routing; owner-mode and supervised admin prompts/tools are wired alternatives. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the system&#39;s standing autonomy/delegation and protected-secret authority boundary, rather than approval of a single task. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: deployment owner; autonomous admin operation inside the envelope does not authorize changing the envelope itself. - Return-to-operation path: confirmed owner choice → policy/config save → gateway restart → subsequent operation under chosen consent/autonomy mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may perform permitted admin work, but cannot decide its own ultimate sandbox/delegation policy. - Evidence: owner-only envelope and protected limits; actual authoritative switch and return; distinct subsequent admin behaviour; chat routes omit sandbox authority. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Linux owner mode has platform-specific constraints. This is deployment-parent policy, not a claim of project-level governance or that ordinary approval cards alone are S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6/assessments/fe/ferrule.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6\">ea30365 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:13:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davesheffer/coding-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Coding Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davesheffer/coding-orchestrator/tree/dca682d7a1bc99c6b83508ad03186b2015085e8a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dca682d7a1bc99c6b83508ad03186b2015085e8a\">dca682d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous coding/research/test work by the host-model agent and installed native scout/runner/builder agents with bounded task/tool loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain repository structure, source changes, failing checks, implementation alternatives and task-local tool feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main session model designs/solves ambiguous work, the scout selects what to investigate within its brief, the builder chooses concrete edits/validation for a specified change, and the runner interprets exact check outcome without changing the task&#39;s scope. - Decision owner: Claude Code/Codex main-session and named-child models reachable through installed first-party role contracts, not the Python installer or preconfigured model strings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed role files, native subagent invocation, explicit model/effort/brief, read-only helper/guard, sandbox checks where supported, typed outcome report and optional Windows launcher. - Closure path: user objective → main model investigation/design and bounded role assignment → child-model tools/read/check/edit feedback → RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED → main model checks, corrects or completes task. - Boundary reachability: default installers put the role definitions and main charter into the selected assistant home; starting a supported Claude/Codex session exposes the named roles. The Windows launcher separately supplies a real restricted CLI fallback, while native host execution is conditional on role discovery and effective permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves static roles, installers, command/report helpers and sandbox policy but no task-specific decisions or coding output. The external client executes inference, while the installed first-party definitions directly shape real normal-operation model roles. - Evidence: role dispatch rules, Claude role routing, builder role, scout role, launch restrictions. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for distributed role construction; provider-authenticated live role selection and effective sandbox enforcement were not run here. - Caveats: an optional Windows launcher cannot be silently assumed to work on Linux/macOS, and mere role model metadata is weaker than observing a live session. The role pack&#39;s own validation record describes these native gaps.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: direct attenuation of overlapping file edits and order-dependent interference among several independent coding S1 subunits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent builders/writers touching one file or repository without disjoint ownership, overlapping edits and approvals during unresolved prior work, and invalid test results produced before a dependency completes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model assigns disjoint paths and bounded briefs to independent subagents, keeps conflicting edits/dependencies sequential, and changes assignment/wait order after builder diffs, check exits and verification indicate overlap or dependency failure. - Decision owner: installed main-session orchestration model, constrained by real user scope and native permission boundaries; deterministic role safety guards are not the coordination decision maker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role separation (scout/critic read-only, runner check-only, builder scoped writing), explicit file-ownership clauses, dependency/wait rules, actual diffs and result reports, host&#39;s task completion notices, and conditional checked sandbox boundaries. - Closure path: proposed independent work may touch the same mutable code or require an earlier fix → main model gives non-overlapping ownership or delays the dependent worker → observed diff/check/report returns → main model corrects assignment or waits/replans → later model-owned S1 edits use the revised scope/order. - Boundary reachability: both installed main-session charters explicitly instruct disjoint file ownership, parallelism only for independent units, sequential edits/dependencies and checking actual child results; native named agents are reachable after installation. No unrelated external work manager is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model decides which changes are independent and how to respond to observed overlap; static role restrictions and a guard may reduce the chance of unsafe writes but cannot select a domain-specific collision resolution. - Distinct S1 units: at least two concurrently or sequentially handed-off native child models (for example separate scout/runner and builder branches, or two scoped builders) working on one coding project under the same main model. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two builders can produce conflicting edits to shared files, while a runner checking an incomplete dependency can report stale results that mislead the later builder/parent. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party parent role contract with disjoint file ownership, specified briefs and ordered dependency/editor actions, plus returned diffs/reports and correction by the main model. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the main model inspects observed builder diffs and real runner exits, then narrows or revises a worker&#39;s file scope, reassigns the fix or delays the next writer until conflicting work is reconciled. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: disjoint ownership and dependent-write/check ordering are aimed at a concrete interference among independently acting code-changing S1 roles, not merely routing routine low-cost tasks. - Evidence: Codex no-overlap/task/dependency contract, Claude task partition, builder scoped writing, Codex named role definition. - Basis: explicit skill-driven inter-writer operating policy. - Confidence: medium for model-owned S2 in correctly followed multi-role operation; no runtime global file-claim database or automatic source-collision detector exists. - Caveats: generic task delegation or read-only scout/critic isolation alone is not S2; this claim rests on distinct changing/observing S1 units and source-overlap prevention/correction through the parent model. A single writer mode does not exercise inter-S1 coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: whole-current project control by the main model over delegated task commitments, uncertainty, verification obligations and risk/acceptance interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing current tasks, unverified work, weak reports, elapsed context, child sandbox failures, failed tests, risk/irreversible actions and incomplete handoffs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main model chooses whether to delegate or handle directly, allocates roles/briefs, analyzes child reports/diffs and risk, requires another check/critic, changes task plan/assignment and accepts or returns work for correction. - Decision owner: installed main-session orchestration model. A real user retains authorization over pushes and irreversible actions, but no distinct parent-governed whole-current S3 mode is inferred from occasional permission approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED reports, role constraints, verification commands, client child-task state, optional Mission Control in-flight UI, agent-report.py, relay context gauge/handoff, Jev route/guard hints and actual client permission enforcement. - Closure path: current parallel child outcomes/changed files, low-confidence reports and check exits → model main-session portfolio judgment → correction/delegation/escalation/critic/pass decision → revised child work, accepted code or bounded new session continuation. - Boundary reachability: the main-session charter is installed and loaded as the normal operating instruction; native role responses return to that session, optional UI/launcher provide further observations. Its job is explicitly design, judgment, verification and final response rather than merely waiting for a model child to finish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static rules and context thresholds advise or constrain; the host model owns what to do with the whole current request and whether evidence is sufficient. A dashboard, hook or permission check does not exercise that judgment alone. - Whole-system current view: main conversation receives each launched child result and task status, and can inspect diffs/checks; optional Mission Control displays the active model/agent timeline and report cards. The optional Windows launcher aggregates its own bounded run reports, not arbitrary independent client sessions. - Current-control decision scope: split/reassign project work, sequence disjoint edits, require direct validation or critic review, stop an unsafe course, and choose release readiness within user-granted authority. - Evidence: Codex parent-owned supervision, Claude parent-owned supervision, Mission Control current observation, agent report helper, role handoff contracts. - Basis: explicit operating ownership, supported by native role/report interfaces. - Confidence: medium-high for supported agent-caller mode. - Caveats: Claude shell restrictions may be merely advisory under inherited permissions; actual client permissions and model availability must be verified on deployment. The Mission Control mod is optional and observability, not an independent controller.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: model-owned independent challenge of risky code changes and correctness/security claims using direct source and verification evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s unsupported confidence, a passing-but-irrelevant test, concurrency/security bugs missed by builder and main agent, and accidental self-review of an asserted fix. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately instantiated critic model with a fresh brief independently judges the exact diff/source and produces SHIP | FIX FIRST | RETHINK findings; the main model then resolves/acts on the evidence before claiming the risky work done. - Decision owner: shipped critic role running as a distinct Claude/Codex model agent for independent audit judgment; main model owns remediation and final acceptance, rather than the builder or an automatic CI lint gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh-context adversarial role prompts, read-only policy (with effective-enforcement caveats), exact diff/base and claim, expanding through callers/error paths/tests, failure scenarios, line-anchored findings, typed verdict and main-session correction loop. - Closure path: builder/main proposes risky change or completion claim → separately briefed critic reads direct changed code and adjacent paths → independent finding/SHIP/FIX FIRST/RETHINK returns → main model verifies, requests changes or documents conscious remaining risk before acceptance. - Boundary reachability: Claude installer places agents/critic.md, Codex installer places the native critic.toml; both installed main charters explicitly require critic review for risky or irreversible work. It does not rely on a repo maintainer PR process or a hypothetical external assessor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic model owns a substantive adversarial audit verdict from complementary direct evidence; the static role manifest/guards and main model&#39;s acceptance are separate functions. The builder&#39;s own test/report is not substituted for an independent critic. - Claim being audited: that a proposed coding change or high-risk operation is correct, safe and supported by the stated check evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: builder or runner returns RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED and main reads the actual work/checks. - Complementary access path: distinct fresh-context critic receives the exact diff/base and claim, reads actual code/callers/error cases/tests without accepting author reasoning, and reports concrete adverse scenarios with file/line evidence. - Independence boundary: critic has separate role, model/prompt/brief and read-only posture, no shared author conclusions as settled truth; practical sandbox isolation depends on actual client/host and may be unverified even when role files state read-only. - Who acts on findings: the main orchestration model investigates blocker/major/minor evidence, orders corrective work, reruns checks or explicitly withholds final acceptance; an automatic critic response does not publish changes. - Evidence: Claude critic charter, Codex critic definition, fresh critic routing/feedback, Claude critic acceptance, native validation boundaries. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for distinct installed critical review path, subject to client runtime configuration. - Caveats: runtime enforcement for Claude critic may remain instruction-level under permissive inherited modes, and no paid critic ran in this assessment. Optional user refusal or unsupported native role availability may prevent a specific deployment from exercising S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed organizational future-environment sensing → adaptation-option generation → decision → returned operating capability loop is supplied for the assessed host coding project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model tiers, provider availability, token use and future tooling change appear as configuration/routing concerns, not a functionally closed S4 strategy system. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 owner is bound to a proactive future adaptation cycle; agent choices over current work and context rollover are S3/continuity decisions. - Decision owner: neither autonomous system-model intelligence authority nor a qualifying distinct parent-governed S4 mode is established within the installed bundle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-routing table and fallback rules, context gauge/rollover, optional Jev classification, agent-report.py, benchmarks/optimization research and human configuration updates. - Closure path: no evidenced future/external distinction → novel adaptation option → authoritative selection → returned change to current system capability. A context threshold may trigger a fresh session but preserves current objectives instead of adapting future organizational capabilities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host model can choose a different helper or consult benchmarks, but the released bundle does not supply a strategic outside-and-then intelligence role and completed adaptation return loop. - Evidence: role/routing and continuity rules, Claude relay instructions, relay code, Jev routing, benchmarks. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: medium-high for examined installation and runtime paths. - Caveats: separate external corporate/user strategy may change the role bundle; the repo&#39;s benchmark and optimization materials are not an installed, agent-owned prospective capability governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous or parent-governed ultimate identity/policy decision and subsequent charter-return loop is installed for the coding system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task scope, authorization, tool/network boundaries, project requirements and model availability are externally configured and handled as task/permission governance, not identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user grants or withholds outward/destructive actions and any optional permission fallback; main agent respects the configured role policy, but cannot adjudicate its own ultimate identity or bind the user/project through an S5 resolution institution. - Decision owner: no legitimate S5 mode owner within the installed runtime is demonstrated. User-owned config/approval remains at an external parent but is not a full S5 function-specific return path merely because permission checks exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed AGENTS/CLAUDE instructions, role sandbox settings, user-owned agent-routing.json exception list, external client permissions, Jev risk gate and optional Hunch outside the actual installed managed block. - Closure path: blocked tool or permission fallback request → user/current-model task decision → resumed/denied particular operation; no defined ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authoritative verdict → binding identity charter update → subsequent operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent following constraints has S1/S3 operating rights but no supreme authority to change the system&#39;s charter. A user can reinstall/change settings, but installing code and occasional approval are not S5 parent governance. - Evidence: Codex role permission and consent boundaries, user-local policy handling, Claude parent authority limits, installed managed-block boundary, opt-in Jev guard. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: high for no shipped identity-level return forum at declared boundary. - Caveats: independent OSS users may each govern their assistant installations; do not aggregate their private settings into an organization-wide S5 or borrow the separate Hunch system as this bundle&#39;s own installed authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20404580837d682e46e9d185d4f7cbf08fdb4888/assessments/co/coding-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20404580837d682e46e9d185d4f7cbf08fdb4888\">2040458 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:04:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CodeAlive-AI/pragmatic-orchestration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pragmatic Orchestration</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CodeAlive-AI/pragmatic-orchestration/tree/cc829d908822978fe7fce4670967525ea3a9a267\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cc829d908822978fe7fce4670967525ea3a9a267\">cc829d9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independent model-directed coding, codebase research and validation inside a bounded delegated task, including normal host-model task work when no external worker is needed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source and requirements, implementation choices, test/tool feedback and partial failures within one assigned workspace and task contract. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the selected external coding-agent model chooses investigation, edits, tools, tests and substantive answer inside its assigned scope; the caller model chooses the task contract and later acceptance but does not precompute every worker action. - Decision owner: configured Codex/Claude/OpenCode/Grok/Devin model actor in each actual delegated CLI session; a supported skill-capable host model owns its own nondelegated task decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: porch delegate agent selection, backend adapters, configured model/profile, prompt transport, exact cwd, session/result artifacts, supervisor/registry, bounded status and final-result delivery; external provider models supply inference rather than Porch-owned hidden capabilities. - Closure path: host model assigns a bounded objective → backend model executes its own task/tool feedback loop → code/research/check result and evidence return through the supervisor → calling model checks/accepts or steers/reassigns unfinished work. - Boundary reachability: SKILL.md routes installed skill-capable callers to the shipped executable porch entrypoint, whose delegate dispatcher, Python supervisor and adapters genuinely start the configured worker; this is an advertised real use path, not a test-only fake agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without a model-driven worker, the wrapper can transport tasks and persist events but cannot select the task-specific actions or produce substantive code; without the caller model, the skill does not itself choose the task contract. - Evidence: installed skill delegation map, delegate launcher, supervisor and adapter invocation, delegate runtime. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for shipped model/task/runtime connection; no live provider invocation was attempted. - Caveats: the actual provider binary, account/quota and selected skill-capable host are external installation dependencies. Do not infer provider-internal multi-agent coordination as Porch S2/S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuation of conflicts between independently writing S1 agents during overlapping project delegation and corrective replacement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent writes to the same mutable checkout, a successor modifying partially written files before its predecessor is actually stopped, and different task scopes modifying each other&#39;s work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model caller gives disjoint task scopes and uses distinct user-authorized workspaces for concurrent writers; when new events/diffs expose conflict or failure, it decides whether to redirect/stop an exact worker, preserve partial changes, or withhold a replacement assignment until the writer is confirmed stopped. - Decision owner: the skill-guided calling model in the supported multi-delegate mode. The user has prior authority over workspace creation; the supervisor/registry only enforces run-addressed lifecycle and transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate user-provided working directories, explicit task scope contracts, run IDs, delegate list/events/status/cancel/steer, stop/acknowledgment verification, VCS status and diff checks, and the first-party overlapping-writer guard in delegation policy. - Closure path: two model worker tasks could contend for one checkout → model caller assigns distinct approved workspaces or keeps the replacement waiting → observes run outcomes/partial diffs → sends corrective steer or cancels and confirms terminal status → only then permits the next conflicting S1 writer to act. - Boundary reachability: the shipped skill has explicit multi-worker/parallel-writer and stop-before-reassignment instructions while porch exposes independent running worker IDs, separate cwd binding, observation and targeted intervention. This is a supported optional multiple-delegate operating arrangement, conditional on user-authorized workspaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the caller model judges actual overlap and which worker/scope to change; the private registry prevents mis-addressed control messages but does not detect or autonomously resolve file conflicts. - Distinct S1 units: at least two independently executing coding agents each with a separate model/tool loop and one assigned project task/working directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous unrelated writers on one checkout can overwrite, intermix or invalidate changes; premature reassignment into a predecessor&#39;s partially modified tree repeats the conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party mandatory separate-workspace/clear-scope policy plus substantive parent checkpointing, explicit stop/verify before conflicting replacement, and corrective steering into subsequent tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: run event/cancellation outcome and inspected repository diff go to the model caller; it changes the next worker&#39;s root/scope, or sends the exact active worker a corrective task before continuing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the skill identifies a concrete inter-writer contention hazard and prescribes an operational anti-overlap/feedback relation among distinct worker loops, not merely a mailbox or job queue. - Evidence: delegate multi-writer and replacement contract, parent skill contract, run-addressed control commands, remote workspace guidance. - Basis: explicit operating contract backed by active run-addressed tools. - Confidence: medium: model-owned preventive and corrective protocol is shipped, but there is no automatic cross-workspace write-collision detector or proof that callers always follow the skill. - Caveats: single-delegate or manually co-located writers do not realize this S2 path. Runtime locks protect metadata/mailboxes, not shared repository writes; user must authorize separate writer workspaces.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-caller regulation of the whole current delegated project workload and commitments through project supervision, intervention and acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled worker progress, wrong task interpretations, partial or failed outcomes, unexpected deviations, competing active work, uncertainty over delivery/steering and incomplete acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the calling model evaluates the active delegation portfolio and substantive evidence, chooses new assignments/reprioritization, corrective steering, cancellation, reassignment and whether work is accepted or requires further verification. - Decision owner: the operating skill-capable host model in its agent-orchestrator mode; CLI lifecycle commands are instruments and user scope constraints remain binding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current run enumeration, events cursors, wait-any group snapshots, per-run complete final texts, exact steer delivery status, deviation journals, verified tests/diffs and optional remote-host status. - Closure path: several active worker status/progress/evidence streams → host model forms current project-wide judgment → targeted steer, cancel, replacement, revised workload or acceptance → subsequent worker execution/commitments and verification change. - Boundary reachability: the installed skill expressly directs the model caller to inspect each worker during its first minute, adapt later supervision, keep the parent turn active, observe several runs and make intervention/acceptance decisions; all named control/observation commands are routed to shipped CLI and supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model discretion determines current priorities, whether the work is useful, scope of corrective action and acceptance. A process exit, heartbeat, quota readout or wait-any timeout alone would not make that judgment. - Whole-system current view: delegate list --active enumerates the private run registry, while wait-any reports complete supplied-group state/timing and events/artifacts provide substantive per-worker evidence; the calling model must retain the project group membership rather than infer it from a global list. - Current-control decision scope: allocate distinct worker contracts, redirect exact active sessions, stop unproductive work, delay conflicting replacement, coordinate completion and accept/reject worker contributions against task criteria. - Evidence: model caller supervision and acceptance contract, group wait and event interfaces, registry control, parent-owned review acceptance. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for model-run supported project supervision, absent end-to-end provider session observation. - Caveats: neither the CLI nor optional read-only UI has autonomous whole-fleet scheduling, and a human issuing an occasional CLI stop is not enough to establish a separate operational parent-governed S3 mode. Periodic checks are a caller obligation, not a built-in timer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent, direct-evidence auditing of implementation/correctness/security claims from worker work, with corrective results fed to the caller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion reports, unsafe patches, stale or invented code quotations, defects not visible in a worker&#39;s self-summary, and one actor rubber-stamping its own work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately launched read-only reviewer model assesses the actual source/diff and reports independently reasoned defects; optionally an independent judge model adjudicates combined discoveries, while the calling model chooses corrective disposition. - Decision owner: one or several review-mode model agents operating under the first-party audit prompt rather than the implementing worker&#39;s instruction/authority, followed by the caller model for remedial action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review code direct file/diff input, role-specific security/correctness and optional specialist passes, read-only review contract, per-pass independent reports, cross-check of quoted code against files, optional deterministic dedup and separate LLM judge, final report delivery. - Closure path: worker claims a change is correct/ready → independent review model directly inspects the file/diff and broadens relevant source context → source-anchored findings reach the caller → caller verifies and commands corrective work, targeted tests, or reasoned acceptance before completion. - Boundary reachability: porch review code basic is an ordinary shipped CLI mode, and review ask/super/ultra are explicit supported modes. The skill instructs the host caller to invoke reviewers separately, supply task contracts, and act on findings; no private maintainer CI reviewer is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct reviewing model agents make risk/finding judgments from independent direct access; quote checking/dedup scripts only validate or combine evidence, while the primary worker cannot rewrite those review judgments through its own completion message. - Claim being audited: a delegated coding agent&#39;s implementation/report or the caller&#39;s proposed code change satisfies task and security/correctness requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing worker&#39;s session events, final answer, own tests and source changes return to its calling model through delegate wait. - Complementary access path: independent review code reader receives current source file/diff plus an explicitly incomplete seed and is required to expand its own repository search; findings carry file/line/quoted-code anchors checked against source. - Independence boundary: reviewers are separately invoked read-only model sessions with dedicated audit roles and denied edits/delegation; they are not the working delegate&#39;s self-attestation. Sharing vendor/model family or an agent-selected initial file seed does not itself guarantee diversity; optional judge is another audit pass, not substitute evidence. - Who acts on findings: the calling orchestration model verifies dispositions against the task contract and steers/assigns corrections; neither basic/specialist report nor automatic judge verdict unilaterally accepts worker changes. - Evidence: review operational mode, specialist review dispatch, quote validation, review workflow stages, task-relative remediation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for operational independence and direct-source audit; no live reviewers were paid/run at the frozen source. - Caveats: optional review must actually be invoked for a particular task; basic/specialists do not have an internal judge, and no automated merge gate forces a review finding to block acceptance. Read-only enforcement varies by backend.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported first-party organizational loop that senses changing external/future conditions, develops strategic alternatives and returns a capability adaptation decision into the whole current system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential changes in future provider quality, coding technology, cost, environment or long-horizon deployment capability are discussed, but not closed through a shipped prospective sensing/adaptation process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not supplied for S4; a caller can manually choose another model/provider or alter configuration, and retrospective session history can inform ad hoc choices, without a distinct future-oriented intelligence owner and returned capability-change loop. - Decision owner: no autonomous or legitimate parent S4 decision owner is established within this distribution boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/profile configuration, quota snapshots, session history stats/flags, advice to periodically inspect agent effectiveness, external-model comparison tips and remote-host provisioning helpers. - Closure path: no demonstrated outside-and-then sensing → alternative-generation → adaptation decision → changed future capability/S3 closure; ordinary task replanning and same-run steering are S3, not S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an optional model&#39;s analysis of past sessions could be extended into strategy, but the installed skill does not bind that analysis to first-party prospective adaptation authority and returned operating-capability change. - Evidence: skill review/delegation modes, session-history tools, configuration and quota, remote-host options. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: medium-high within current shipped operational paths. - Caveats: a user-built future-planning program or another organization&#39;s S4 could consume Porch telemetry, but is not a supplied first-party S4 constructor merely because telemetry exists.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity-level or ultimate-policy adjudication mechanism with legitimate decisive ownership and returned governance of Porch operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing permissible operating boundaries, provider identities, security exposure and project goals are limited by human-owned installation/configuration/task contracts, not resolved by a Porch ultimate-policy institution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller/user chooses task scope, external agent accounts, writable roots and provider configuration; per-run permission errors and acceptance decisions do not become identity-level policy arbitration. - Decision owner: no supported autonomous S5 owner or closed parent-governed S5 mode for the whole assessed system is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review read-only prompt, role/tool restrictions where provider supports them, external credentials/config, remote host security hardening, user authorization before parallel writer workspaces and ordinary acceptance criteria. - Closure path: provider permission refusal or task-scope concern → caller/user decision over that task; no shipped identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate top-level arbitration → authoritative update → subsequent Porch charter enforcement loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Porch has operational guardrails and user-set credentials/roots; a model applying those constraints does not author its own ultimate identity, and the parent can configure the tool without performing a defined S5 return loop. - Evidence: skill operating limits, delegate authorization/workspace contract, configuration, remote host security. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: high for absence of a defined identity authority path among inspected shipped runtime and policy surfaces. - Caveats: maintainers and users can revise the published skill, but contribution governance and configuration change are not a first-party operational S5 closure at this system recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ca22ec1154f38dc3128c0d2258d453ee0ab2644c/assessments/pr/pragmatic-orchestration.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ca22ec1154f38dc3128c0d2258d453ee0ab2644c\">ca22ec1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:55:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aeven-AI/Aeven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aeven</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aeven-AI/Aeven/tree/50e27b33594b7f0400fc666668fc1026c3fe7ca1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50e27b33594b7f0400fc666668fc1026c3fe7ca1\">50e27b3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry user tasks through first-party model/tool and continuable child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user objectives, tool failures, incomplete artifacts, interruptions and missing context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next substantive tool/action, delegation or response from observed results. - Decision owner: The Aeven session model actor, with first-party spawned/forked child actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cordis plugin composition, queued turn/step driver, tool execution, durable session logs, filesystem/shell/browser/MCP and host permission gates. - Closure path: Session log → request assembly → model tool calls → execution results → next step; child messages/reporting return to the parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The native loop owns iterative execution. Disabled Codex/Claude subagent rows and provider-internal control are not borrowed. - Evidence: packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; packages/core/agent-loop/src/agent.ts; packages/core/agent-loop/src/tool-calls.ts; packages/subagent/tool-subagent/src/index.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: The released aeven preset mounts normal tools, native spawn/fork delegation and the first-party session loop; model/provider configuration completes ordinary deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent writable interference between parallel work actors in the supported installed swarm/best-of-N mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent workers or competing attempts would otherwise share writable artifacts and contaminate each other before selection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose nonoverlapping slices/separate output locations, keep attempts independent and decide subsequent retries/integration from returned evidence. - Decision owner: The parent model following the installed shipped skill; native subagent actors execute the separated briefs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: avn-swarm and avn-best-of-n packaged instructions, runtime subagents/workflow tools, filesystem/worktree commands and evidence-bearing reports. - Closure path: Parent allocates nonoverlapping briefs and private write locations → children act in their assigned locations → evidence/commit identity is checked → wrong/no-evidence results are rejected and respawned once or retained as gaps, then parent integrates accepted work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is explicit conflict attenuation with an agent-owned allocation/return policy, not S2 from generic messaging. No Agent Teams experimental service is needed. - Evidence: packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-swarm/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-best-of-n/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/README.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/catalog/index.json; packages/host/account-integrations/src/index.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional account-installed catalog skills, not an always-active default guarantee; constraints are agent instructions, not filesystem sandbox proof.. - Boundary reachability: The official release skill catalog contains both ZIP bundles; Settings installCatalogSkill imports the bundled skill into account skills, which the supported session can load. Local bundle bytes were checked equal to authored instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current descendant commitments and intervene on behalf of the root task organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Running/idle/ready descendants, corrupt or unavailable records, delayed work and tasks needing redirection or interruption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which current descendant work to interrupt and which direct child to steer or continue after inspecting current state. - Decision owner: The ancestor/root model actor within lineage-bound runtime authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents descendants projection, live/durable status, sendmessage, interruptagent, completion notification and authoritative lineage checks. - Closure path: Current tree/status/report → parent selects intervention/continuation → subagent service routes steering at a step boundary or requests cancellation → changed child activity and later reports return to the root. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The narrow credited function is current commitment regulation over the root task tree. It is not S3 from spawning alone, registry existence or a deterministic cancellation API without the model owner. - Evidence: packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/src/list-agents.ts; packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/src/index.ts; packages/subagent/subagent/src/control.ts; packages/subagent/subagent/src/continuation.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Interrupt is asynchronous, stops only the target current turn and does not stop its children or drain queued messages. No human parent modifier is inferred from browser steering.. - Boundary reachability: The default aeven preset mounts list/control alongside continuable spawn/fork children. These tools use the live calling agent and recorded lineage; no custom supervisor or experimental Team layer is needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement author claims with an independent raw-diff review and a returned correction/re-review loop in the installed review skill mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported completion can hide bugs, wrong callers or goal failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The fresh reviewer challenges the actual code/diff; the author accepts or rebuts findings with primary evidence, fixes accepted findings and returns the changed artifact for review. - Decision owner: Separate fresh reviewer model plus author model with explicitly separated review/edit rights; a human resolves only an unresolved terminal dispute. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: avn-review-loop packaged brief, fixed base/diff, raw code/tests, per-finding ledger, four-round bound and no-edit reviewer rule. - Closure path: Author result → fresh-context read-only reviewer inspects raw changed code → APPROVE/CHANGES with file:line claims → author fixes/rebuts and reruns checks → reviewer rechecks open IDs/changed code; failures/timeouts are not approval. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The independently framed critic has materially different access to the artifact rather than repeating author prose; both judgment and returned correction are packaged instructions using supplied model actors. Deterministic CI and routine Ralph reports are not the credited auditor. - Evidence: packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-review-loop/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/catalog/index.json; packages/skill/skill-catalog/README.md; packages/host/account-integrations/src/index.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional installed skill mode, not automatic S3 for every session. Actual run compliance or independent approval is not asserted by this source assessment.. - Boundary reachability: The release catalog ships avn-review-loop.zip, verified byte-identical to its authored SKILL.md. Supported Settings/account installation and skill loading make the workflow reachable without custom review code; it can use the default fresh spawn provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective harness adaptation function is supplied at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The environmental/authority variety potentially addressed by the inspected paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right established in the assessed supported distribution. - Decision owner: No qualifying agent or parent owner established for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Background procedure distillation, user correction/slowness feedback, memory recall, capability candidates, lessons-to-checks and disabled self-extension tools. - Closure path: No corresponding function-specific organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped learning path derives reusable procedures from completed tasks and corrections; it does not itself investigate prospective environmental alternatives or arbitrate them with present capability. Tool/web research remains S1 task execution unless a distinct adaptation case/return is established. Source-only developer self-extension and experimental packages do not donate an S4 mode. - Evidence: packages/capability/tool-capability/src/learner.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/src/recall.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/src/index.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/README.md; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-lessons/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity or ultimate-policy arbitration loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The environmental/authority variety potentially addressed by the inspected paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right established in the assessed supported distribution. - Decision owner: No qualifying agent or parent owner established for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persona/preferences, permission approvals, plan/goal requests, plugin/preset configuration and optional skill installation. - Closure path: No corresponding function-specific organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These define or configure the current assistant and authorize ordinary tasks/extensions; no runtime identity/ultimate-policy dispute is routed to a legitimate final authority and returned as a function-specific governing decision. Maintainer governance of Aeven releases is adjacent, not an operational S5 owner. - Evidence: packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; docs/architecture.md; packages/core/agent-loop/src/agent.ts; packages/experimental/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06fccd0f6b1a06204de177306ddf2f00e96abdf0/assessments/ae/aeven.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06fccd0f6b1a06204de177306ddf2f00e96abdf0\">06fccd0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
